{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8d9cb5f6205125b2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tHybridSB-MoE: Dual-Domain Schrödinger Bridges\n2\twith Scene-Adaptive Expert Routing for Speech\n3\tEnhancement\n4\t\n5\tarXiv:2608.12715v1 [cs.SD] 13 Aug 2026\n6\t\n7\tZhengyi Lu Aswini Sivakumar Jie Hu Yao Qiang\n8\tDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering, Oakland University\n9\tRochester, MI 48309\n10\t{zhengyilu, aswinisivakumar, jiehu, qiang}@oakland.edu\n11\t\n12\tAbstract\n13\tGenerative speech enhancement faces three gaps: spectral models capture harmonic\n14\tstructure but often disrupt phase, waveform models preserve phase but miss harmonics, and Schrödinger Bridges (SB) shorten transport from noise to clean speech\n15\tbut leave inference cost only loosely tied to training. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\n16\ta dual-domain framework that fills these gaps through three contributions unified\n17\tby a single asymmetric design principle. (i) Asymmetric uncertainty fusion: The\n18\tspectral path captures epistemic uncertainty via expert disagreement, while the\n19\twaveform bridge models aleatoric variance through stochastic dynamics. We fuse\n20\tthem asymmetrically, allowing the mixing weight to adapt to distinct error regimes\n21\trather than average predictions. (ii) Heterogeneous MoE with top-k=2 routing\n22\tacross five distinct architectural archetypes, where architectural diversity makes the\n23\tepistemic signal indicate which inductive bias fails rather than small perturbations\n24\tamong similar experts. (iii) Discretization bound (Theorem 1): path-consistency\n25\tand trajectory regularizers together bound the K-step bridge sampling error in\n26\t2-Wasserstein distance at rate K −α , making small-K inference an objective-level\n27\tguarantee rather than an empirical claim. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, HybridSBMoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at their step budgets while\n28\tremaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods.\n29\t\n30\t1\n31\t\n32\tIntroduction\n33\t\n34\tSpeech enhancement (SE) has long faced a structural domain trade-off. Spectral methods are\n35\teffective at modeling harmonic structure and stationary noise, but often fragment phase across STFT\n36\tframes; waveform methods preserve phase continuity, but can struggle with harmonically structured\n37\tinterference (Fig. 1). This trade-off becomes especially limiting in real-world deployment, where\n38\ta hearing aid on a moving bus, a video-conferencing system in a crowded café, or an in-car voice\n39\tassistant must handle harmonic engine drone, broadband ambient hum, and non-stationary crowd\n40\tbabble under millisecond latency budgets and without scene labels at inference time [1, 2]. These\n41\tsettings require more than a universally stronger model: they require a mechanism that can exploit\n42\tcomplementary inductive biases and decide which one to trust for each input.\n43\tA decade of deep learning has advanced SE from DNN masking [3, 4], through time-domain\n44\t[5, 2, 6] and spectro-temporal [7, 8] architectures to diffusion-based generative models [9, 10, 11] and\n45\tSchrödinger Bridge formulations that replace the uninformative Gaussian prior of diffusion with direct\n46\ttransport from noisy to clean speech. Despite this progress, three structural limitations persist that are\n47\tprecisely what a dual-domain framework must address. (i) Single-domain commitment. Existing\n48\tmethods typically operate in either the waveform or spectral domain, sacrificing the complementary\n49\tinductive bias of the other [8]. (ii) Uniform processing across heterogeneous noise. A single\n50\tPreprint.\n51\t\n52\t\fnetwork handles stationary appliance hum, harmonic engine noise, and non-stationary crowd babble\n53\talike, despite their structurally different inductive biases [12, 13]. (iii) Loosely controlled sampling\n54\tcost. Generative SE pipelines often require many iterative refinement steps, with limited formal\n55\tconnection between the training objective and the inference budget [10, 11].\n56\tThe natural response, i.e., running a spectral and a waveform pathway in parallel and combining\n57\tthem, fails on its own, because a generic ensemble of two prediction streams provides no mechanism\n58\tto know which pathway is failing on a given input. Without such signal, the combination defaults\n59\tto fixed-weight averaging, which is provably suboptimal whenever the two branches’ errors are\n60\tuncorrelated, and the dual-domain promise reduces to a parameter-count increase.\n61\tWe introduce HybridSB-MoE, a dual-domain framework organized around one asymmetric idea: a multiexpert spectral path naturally produces an epistemic uncertainty signal (which expert is right?), and a stochastic\n62\twaveform bridge naturally produces an aleatoric one\n63\t(intrinsic transport noise). These are categorically different signals about categorically different errors. Pairing\n64\tthe two pathways and fusing on this asymmetry yields\n65\ta mixing weight that selects between two error regimes\n66\trather than averaging two predictions, transforming the\n67\tdual-domain promise into a principled co-design. Two\n68\tfurther architectural choices are required for this idea to\n69\twork. First, the spectral pathway must produce informative expert disagreement, which requires architectural Figure 1: The persistent dual-domain diheterogeneity rather than capacity-replicated variation. chotomy in speech enhancement. WaveWe therefore use heterogeneous expert archetypes, each form processing preserves temporal fine\n70\taligned with a canonical noise-processing primitive, in- structure and phase coherence but undercluding low-rank denoising, wide receptive fields, in- performs on harmonically structured information bottlenecks, harmonic bases, and universal terference; spectral processing captures\n71\tapproximation, and combine them under sparse routing. harmonic structure and stationary noise\n72\tSecond, the waveform pathway must remain faithful un- patterns but fragments phase across STFT\n73\tder the small inference budget K targeted by the system. frames. The two domains succeed and fail\n74\tWe therefore train it with path-consistency and trajectory in opposite regimes, and existing generaregularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that jointly min- tive methods inherit one half of the tradeimizing them bounds the K-step bridge discretization off by committing to a single domain.\n75\terror in 2-Wasserstein distance, making small-K inference a consequence of the training objective\n76\trather than an empirical heuristic. In Section 3, we further show that no proper subset of these three\n77\tcomponents recovers the central design property.\n78\tOur contributions are as follows:\n79\t1. A dual-domain framework built around asymmetric uncertainty. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\n80\ta dual-domain framework that couples spectral MoE and waveform SB pathways. By pairing each\n81\tpath with its natural uncertainty signal, epistemic disagreement for spectral experts and aleatoric\n82\tvariance for the waveform bridge, HybridSB-MoE enables fusion that adapts to error regimes rather\n83\tthan fixed-weight averaging.\n84\t2. A heterogeneous spectral MoE for informative epistemic disagreement. We design a sparse\n85\ttop-k=2 spectral MoE with architecturally distinct expert archetypes. This heterogeneity makes\n86\texpert disagreement reflect mismatched inductive biases rather than small perturbations among\n87\tcapacity-replicated experts.\n88\t3. A regularized waveform bridge for few-step inference. We train the waveform SB with\n89\tpath consistency and trajectory regularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that they bound the K-step\n90\tdiscretization error in 2-Wasserstein distance, linking small-K inference to the training objective.\n91\t4. SOTA on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE improves over diffusion- and SB-based\n92\tbaselines at their respective step budgets, remains competitive with consistency-distilled few-step\n93\tmethods, and provides calibrated fusion uncertainty for input-adaptive dual-domain enhancement.\n94\t\n95\t2\n96\t\n97\t\f2\n98\t\n99\tRelated Work\n100\t\n101\tDiscriminative SE. The field has progressed from DNN masking [3, 4] that surpassed classical\n102\tWiener and MMSE estimators [14, 15], through time-domain encoder–decoders (Conv-TasNet [5, 16],\n103\tDEMUCS [2]), dual-path sequence models [6], full-sub-band hybrids (FullSubNet+ [7]), spectrotemporal grids (TF-GridNet [8]), and recent Mamba-based linear-complexity backbones [17, 18].\n104\tJoint enhancement objectives that combine SI-SDR with perceptual surrogates push fidelity further\n105\t[19, 20], yet the underlying paradigm remains a one-shot deterministic regression. Two limitations\n106\ttherefore persist regardless of architecture. (i) Inherent ambiguity: under severe non-stationary\n107\tor harmonically structured noise, the noisy-to-clean mapping admits multiple plausible solutions,\n108\tand a deterministic regressor must collapse this multimodal posterior to a single point estimate,\n109\twhich typically manifests as residual noise or over-suppressed harmonics [21]. (ii) No uncertainty:\n110\tdiscriminative networks expose no signal at inference time to flag this ambiguity, so a downstream\n111\tmodule cannot tell when the prediction should be trusted—making them unsuitable as one branch of\n112\ta confidence-routed dual-domain combiner.\n113\tGenerative models and Schrödinger Bridges for SE. Likelihood-based [21] and score-based\n114\t[9, 22, 23] diffusion models reverse a noise corruption process; SGMSE/SGMSE+ [10, 11] apply\n115\tthis in the complex spectrogram domain, jointly modeling magnitude and phase, but require many\n116\titerative steps from an uninformative Gaussian prior. Two recent paradigms exploit the structure of\n117\tthe noisy observation directly. Flow-matching approaches [24, 25] regress velocity fields between\n118\tcoupled distributions, shortening the sampling trajectory but typically without an explicit stochastic\n119\tperturbation. Schrödinger Bridges [26, 27] learn the optimal-transport-like coupling between the\n120\tnoisy observation and clean speech, retaining a small bridge variance for full-support marginals.\n121\tSB for SE [28, 29, 30, 31, 32] confirm improved low-SNR structure preservation along this shorter\n122\tpath. Empirical step-count reductions have been achieved by consistency distillation [33, 34],\n123\tadversarial few-step training [35], and SB-consistency trajectory models [36]. None of these works\n124\tlinks the training objective to the inference budget, where small K is asserted but not derived.\n125\tTheoretical convergence analyses for diffusion sampling exist in the broader literature [37], but their\n126\tbounds are stated in terms of the score-matching error rather than tractable, training-time-minimized\n127\tregularizers, and have not been adapted to the doubly-conditioned SB setting. Moreover, these\n128\tmethods remain single-domain and lack calibrated uncertainty for fusion. We address both with\n129\tasymmetric uncertainty fusion and a discretization bound (Theorem 1) that links small-K inference\n130\tto explicit training-time quantities.\n131\tMixture-of-Experts for SE. MoE enables conditional computation with sparse routing, scaling\n132\tcapacity without proportional inference cost [38, 39, 40]. In SE, sparse MoE [12] first enabled\n133\tscene-adaptive processing via noise-clustered experts; zero-shot personalization [41] extended this to\n134\tspeakers via embedding-based routing; clean-cluster pre-training [13] enforced expert specialization\n135\tthrough pre-training initialization; and dynamic capacity allocation [42] improved routing efficiency\n136\tat inference. However, this line of work shares three limitations. (i) Homogeneity. Existing methods\n137\treplicate a single architecture across experts, assuming one computational primitive can handle tonal,\n138\tambient, harmonic, and crowd noise. This assumption breaks down under realistic, structurally\n139\tdiverse noise. (ii) Routing without uncertainty. Existing routing only partitions inputs into specialist\n140\tregions, rather than exposing interpretable uncertainty; thus, the epistemic signal from multi-expert\n141\tdisagreement remains unused. (iii) Separation from generative SE. MoE routing and generative bridge\n142\tmodels remain disconnected in SE, leaving scene-adaptive computation and structured distributional\n143\ttransport as separate lines of work. We address all three with heterogeneous archetype experts that\n144\tencode distinct noise-specific inductive biases. Their disagreement provides the epistemic signal\n145\twhich, together with the SB’s aleatoric variance, drives asymmetric fusion. To our knowledge, this is\n146\tthe first joint MoE–SB framework for SE.\n147\tUncertainty quantification and dual-branch fusion. Calibrated uncertainty for deep regression is\n148\tmost commonly obtained via deep ensembles [43] or learned heteroscedastic variance heads, with\n149\tclassification calibration assessed via ECE [44]. Prior dual-branch SE methods that combine timeand frequency-domain estimates typically use either fixed-weight averaging or learned attention over\n150\tpredictions [8, 2]; none assigns categorically distinct uncertainty types to the two branches, and so the\n151\tfusion cannot route on which kind of error each branch is exposed to. Our pathway-typed asymmetric\n152\tfusion is, to our knowledge, the first to exploit this structure, and the calibration loss (Eq. (11)) ties\n153\tthe abstract epistemic/aleatoric labels to measurable per-pathway reconstruction errors so the routing\n154\tweight is learned, not asserted.\n155\t3\n156\t\n157\t\fFigure 2: Overview of HybridSB-MoE. The spectral pathway (top) routes log-magnitude features\n158\tz = log |S{y}| through N heterogeneous archetype experts via gating G(z) to produce xspec (t).\n159\tThe waveform pathway (bottom) iteratively refines an SB state [xt , y] through a U-Net to produce\n160\txwave (t). An uncertainty-aware fusion combines both via uepi (top-k expert disagreement) and uale\n161\t(bridge-variance head) to yield x̂(t).\n162\t\n163\t3\n164\t\n165\tMethod\n166\t\n167\tHybridSB-MoE has three coupled components: a heterogeneous spectral MoE, an SB waveform\n168\tpathway with path-consistency and trajectory regularizers (for which we prove a K-step discretization\n169\tbound, Theorem 1), and an asymmetric uncertainty fusion that selects between epistemic and aleatoric\n170\terror regimes. Figure 2 illustrates the overall HybridSB-MoE framework. We develop each component\n171\tin turn and close the section by showing that no proper subset recovers the central design property.\n172\tOverview. Given a noisy input y(t), HybridSB-MoE processes it through two parallel pathways\n173\tdesigned to capture complementary speech characteristics. The spectral pathway applies the heterogeneous MoE to the log-magnitude STFT for scene-adaptive enhancement. In parallel, the waveform\n174\tpathway runs an efficient SB on the time-domain signal to preserve phase. The two pathways exhibit\n175\tasymmetric reliability across acoustic conditions: spectral processing excels in harmonically structured noise, whereas waveform processing is more robust in phase-sensitive scenarios. Consequently,\n176\ta fixed fusion strategy is inherently suboptimal. We therefore introduce an uncertainty-aware fusion\n177\tmodule that integrates xspec (t) and xwave (t) by dynamically weighting each pathway based on\n178\tconfidence, producing the final enhanced signal x̂(t). Both pathways run in parallel at inference, and\n179\tlong utterances use overlap-add segmentation (Appendix B).\n180\tSpectral feature extraction. We apply the STFT [45] to obtain the complex spectrogram S{y} ∈\n181\tCF ×Tf (F frequency bins) and decompose it in polar form into magnitude |S{y}| and phase ∠S{y},\n182\tprocessed by separate heads (below). The MoE consumes log-magnitude features z = log |S{y}| ∈\n183\tRF ×Tf , whose logarithmic scaling compresses the dynamic range in line with human hearing.\n184\tHeterogeneous mixture-of-experts. Unlike conventional homogeneous MoE designs [38, 39, 40] in\n185\twhich all experts share the same architecture and differ only in learned weights, we instantiate distinct\n186\tarchitectural archetypes, each tailored to a canonical noise-processing primitive, i.e., Home, Nature,\n187\tOffice, Transport, and Public for VoiceBank+DEMAND (see Appendix A for full specification).\n188\tUnder sparse top-k routing, pairwise mixtures and weighted blends of these archetypes cover the 14\n189\tnoise categories in VoiceBank+DEMAND [46] combinatorially rather than by a dedicated expert\n190\tper noise, and the design extends to new acoustic environments by adding archetypes per noise\n191\tfamily encountered, without altering the rest of the framework (see Appendix A for details). All\n192\texperts share a backbone of normalization (group norm [47]) → variable internal modules →\n193\tbottleneck projection, ensuring routing-interface compatibility while permitting structurally divergent\n194\tintermediate computation. This design, a small semantically anchored basis composed of sparse\n195\t4\n196\t\n197\t\frouting, replaces the parameter scaling of large homogeneous MoEs with interpretable routing and\n198\tmeaningful expert disagreement, which we use for asymmetric fusion below.\n199\tScene-adaptive sparse routing. Standard sparse routing [38, 39] assigns each input token to its\n200\ttop-k experts independently, which is well-suited to language modelling but ill-suited to SE: the\n201\tdominant noise type is a global property of an utterance, while local acoustic events (a phoneme onset,\n202\ta transient hit) require frame-level adaptivity. We therefore use a two-level gate: Archetype-level\n203\trouting Garch (z) pools z across time and decides the dominant archetype mixture for the whole\n204\tutterance; token-level routing Gtoken (z) refines this assignment per frame. The two combine as\n205\tG(z) = αGarch (z) + (1 − α)Gtoken (z) with α ∈ [0, 1]. An MLP maps G(z) to per-expert logits;\n206\tthe top-k entries are softmax-renormalized to weights {Gi (z)}i∈Ik , and the routed output is\n207\tX\n208\tx̂spec =\n209\tGi (z) Ei (z),\n210\t(1)\n211\ti∈Ik\n212\t\n213\twhere Ik indexes the selected experts and Ei are expert networks. To prevent expert collapse [38, 39],\n214\ta standard load-balancing regularizer\n215\tLaux = λI Var(pi ) + λL Var(ni )\n216\t\n217\t(2)\n218\t\n219\tpenalizes uneven utilization, where pi and ni denote the average routing probability and token count\n220\tof expert i, respectively.\n221\tSpectral reconstruction. The routed features x̂spec pass through two parallel 1 × 1-conv heads.\n222\tSince phase cannot be inferred from log-magnitude alone, the phase head also consumes the noisy\n223\tphase as (sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y}), concatenated along the channel dimension:\n224\tM̂ = Mmax sigmoid(hmag (x̂spec )), ∆ϕ = ϕmax tanh(hpha (x̂spec , sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y})), (3)\n225\twhere Mmax , ϕmax bound the mask and phase correction to prevent over-suppression, excessive\n226\tamplification, and phase-wrap instability. The enhanced spectrum Ŝ = M̂ ⊙ |S{y}| · ej(∠S{y}+∆ϕ)\n227\tis inverted to xspec (t) via iSTFT with overlap-add. STFT processing nevertheless fragments temporal\n228\tfine structure through frame-wise phase decoupling, motivating the parallel waveform pathway below.\n229\tSchrödinger Bridge formulation. The continuous-time SB problem seeks the stochastic process\n230\tminimizing the KL divergence to a reference Brownian motion subject to fixed marginal distributions\n231\tat both endpoints [26, 27, 48]. In our application the two endpoints are the noisy observation y\n232\tand the clean target x, so the bridge directly couples the conditional distributions our system must\n233\ttransport between. Where standard diffusion [9] traverses the full path from a data-independent\n234\tGaussian prior, the bridge exploits the substantial mutual information between y and x to yield a\n235\tshorter, more informative trajectory and, empirically, fewer sampling steps and better low-SNR\n236\tstructure preservation [28, 29, 30]. Conceptually adjacent flow-matching methods [24, 25] also\n237\tcouple paired distributions but typically without an explicit bridge perturbation; our SB construction\n238\tretains a small noise term σt ϵ to keep the marginal p(xt | x, y) full-support, which is required for\n239\tscore-based reverse sampling and for the trajectory regularizer to act as a self-consistency constraint.\n240\tThe intermediate state at diffusion time t ∈ [0, T ] interpolates between y and x with a small Gaussian\n241\tperturbation,\n242\tq\n243\tq\n244\txt =\n245\t\n246\tβ̄t x +\n247\t\n248\t1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ,\n249\t\n250\tϵ ∼ N (0, I),\n251\t\n252\t(4)\n253\t\n254\twhere β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 ((t/T +s)/(1+s)·π/2) is a cumulative cosine p\n255\tschedule [49]\n256\tdecreasing monotonically from β̄0 = 1 to β̄T = 0 (offset s=0.008), and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t )\n257\t(σmax =0.05) vanishes at both endpoints, so x0 = x and xT = y deterministically while σt ϵ supplies\n258\tthe full-support marginal p(xt | x, y) required by the score-based reverse process.\n259\tThe reverse process is parameterized as a learnable denoiser x̂θ (xt , y, t) that predicts the clean target\n260\tdirectly from the bridge state and the observation. Rather than solving the reverse-time stochastic\n261\tdifferential equation (SDE) associated with the bridge in closed form, we adopt a tractable bridgeconsistent update that iteratively re-injects the predicted clean estimate into the forward construction\n262\tof Eq. (4) at the previous timestep:\n263\tq\n264\tq\n265\txt−1 = β̄t−1 x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t−1 y + σt−1 z,\n266\tz ∼ N (0, I).\n267\t(5)\n268\t5\n269\t\n270\t\fThis data-prediction update is the bridge analogue of DDPM/DDIM ancestral sampling [9, 23]: deterministic when σt−1 =0, stochastic otherwise. Bridge-marginal consistency is enforced empirically\n271\tthrough the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced below. The denoiser is a 1D\n272\tU-Net with transformer bottleneck and FiLM timestep conditioning (Appendix B). The training\n273\tobjective is the data-prediction loss:\n274\t2\n275\t\n276\tLdata\n277\tSB = Et,(x,y),ϵ x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x 2 ,\n278\t\n279\t(6)\n280\t\n281\taugmented by the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced next.\n282\tPath-consistency and trajectory regularizers. A fixed front-loaded schedule tk = T (k/K)γ with\n283\tγ<1 concentrates the K-step budget in the early-trajectory regime where the SNR changes fastest and\n284\twhere errors propagate to all later steps [9]. Two regularizers then encourage a smooth, low-curvature\n285\tdenoising path in the (y, x) subspace. The path-consistency loss enforces cross-timestep agreement\n286\tof clean-signal predictions along the same trajectory:\n287\th\n288\ti\n289\t2\n290\tLpath = Et,t′ ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ )∥\n291\t(7)\n292\twhere t, t′ are independently sampled along the same bridge trajectory. This penalizes inconsistent\n293\tclean-signal predictions across timesteps, reducing reverse-process curvature (cf. consistency models\n294\t[34], but here for path smoothing, not distillation). The trajectory loss anchors xt to the scheduleconsistent reconstruction:\n295\t\"\n296\t#\n297\tq\n298\tq\n299\t\u0001 2\n300\tLtraj = Et xt −\n301\tβ̄t x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t y\n302\t.\n303\t(8)\n304\tAt the optimum x̂θ = x, the residual reduces to σt ϵ, so Ltraj couples the prediction to the specific\n305\tbridge state xt at each timestep, complementing the in-expectation regression of Eq. (6). At inference,\n306\twe use K=8 steps with the front-loaded non-uniform discretization tk = T (k/K)γ , γ=0.6 (see\n307\tAppendix B for more details).\n308\tDiscretization bound. The two regularizers above make small-K inference viable, and we now show\n309\twhy. Let p̂K be the law of the K-step rollout of Eq. (5) at t=0 and pbr\n310\t0 the continuous-time bridge\n311\tmarginal at t=0, both conditional on y.\n312\tAssumption 1 (Regularity).\n313\tp (i) x̂θ (·, y, t) is Lx -Lipschitz in its first argument and Lt -Lipschitz in\n314\tt. (ii) β̄t and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t ) are C 1 on [0, T ], with β̄t monotone and bounded second\n315\tderivative. (iii) E∥xt ∥2 and E∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥2 are uniformly bounded in t.\n316\tTheorem 1 (K-step bridge discretization bound). Under Assumption 1, with the schedule tk =\n317\tT (k/K)γ , γ ∈ (0, 1],\n318\tq\n319\t\u0001\n320\t−α\n321\tW2 p̂K , pbr\n322\t≤\n323\tC\n324\tK\n325\t+\n326\tC\n327\tL⋆path + L⋆traj ,\n328\t(9)\n329\t1\n330\t2\n331\t0\n332\twith α = min(1, γ), L⋆ the training-termination values of Eq. (7)–(8), and C1 , C2 depending only\n333\ton Lx , Lt , σmax , and the curvature of β̄t .\n334\tWith α = min(1, γ), front-loading (γ < 1) concentrates steps near t=0 where Φ′ is largest, which\n335\tis what drives small-K behavior empirically.\n336\tEach term corresponds to a separate lever: C1 K −α is\n337\t√\n338\tcontrolled by inference-time K, γ; C2 L⋆ is controlled by training-time minimization of the two\n339\t⋆\n340\tregularizers and is independent of K.\n341\t√ Two consequences follow. First, once L is small, the bound\n342\t−α\n343\tsaturates: any K with C1 K ≲ C2 L⋆ already attains the asymptotic floor, which is what justifies\n344\tour K=8 rather than the much larger K of standard diffusion sampling. Second, the regularizers\n345\tare not auxiliary terms; without them, the second term has no training-time upper bound, and the\n346\tinequality does not exist as a function of K. Therefore, removing either breaks the entire argument.\n347\tThis is the formal sense in which path-consistency and trajectory regularization are load-bearing\n348\tfor our inference schedule. Eq. (9) is closely related to recent convergence results for diffusion\n349\tsampling [37] but adapts those analyses to the doubly-conditioned bridge process and, importantly,\n350\treplaces score-matching error with the explicit, training-time-minimized regularizer pair, which\n351\tmakes the bound directly actionable as a design tool. The full proof includes a one-step fidelity\n352\tlemma combined with a non-uniform Riemann estimate under a synchronous coupling, with details\n353\tdeferred to Appendix C. We present Eq. (9) as a design-justifying inequality, not a tight predictor.\n354\t6\n355\t\n356\t\fAsymmetric uncertainty fusion. A naive dual-branch ensembler treats the two pathways symmetrically; ours does not, by design. Epistemic uncertainty (‘which expert is right?’) is well-defined only\n357\twhen multiple experts coexist (the spectral MoE); the waveform pathway has no analog. Aleatoric\n358\tuncertainty (intrinsic stochasticity of the bridge SDE in Eq. (4)) is well-defined only when the\n359\tforward process is genuinely stochastic (the waveform pathway); the deterministic spectral mask in\n360\tEq. (3) has no analog. Each pathway therefore carries exactly one uncertainty type: uepi flags an\n361\tarchitectural mismatch, uale flags a stochastic transport error. The fusion selects\n362\tP between these two\n363\terror regimes; it does not average two predictions. Concretely, uepi = kT1 f i∈Ik ∥Ei (z)− Ē(z)∥22\n364\tP\n365\twith Ē(z) = k1 i∈Ik Ei (z) (deep-ensembles sense [43], not Bayesian posterior); uale is from the\n366\tU-Net’s per-sample log-variance head, exponentiated and time-averaged. After z-score normalization,\n367\ta 2-layer MLP gives w = σ(MLP(ũepi , ũale )) ∈ [0, 1], and\n368\tx̂(t) = w · xspec (t) + (1 − w) · xwave (t).\n369\t\n370\t(10)\n371\t\n372\tHigh expert disagreement pushes w toward the waveform pathway and high SB variance toward the\n373\tspectral pathway: each pathway is deferred away from when its native error is large. A calibration\n374\tloss anchors the two scalars to the errors they should track,\n375\tLcal = (uepi − ∥xspec − x∥22 )2 + (uale − ∥xwave − x∥22 )2 ,\n376\t\n377\t(11)\n378\t\n379\twhere uepi , uale are pre-normalized; without it, the scalars have no incentive to scale with error, and\n380\tthe fusion’s dependence on them would be arbitrary.\n381\tTraining. All components are trained end-to-end. Each step runs both pathways in parallel: the\n382\tspectral path produces xspec , uepi ; the waveform path samples t, ϵ, forms xt via Eq. (4), and outputs\n383\txwave , uale . The fusion yields x̂. The objective combines reconstruction, SB, load-balancing, and\n384\tcalibration losses:\n385\tL = Lrec + λSB LSB + λaux Laux + λcal Lcal ,\n386\t(12)\n387\t2\n388\tdata\n389\twith Lrec = ∥x̂ − x∥2 and LSB = LSB + λpath Lpath + λtraj Ltraj . Loss weights (gridsearched on a 10% VB split): λSB =1.0, λpath =0.1, λtraj =0.05, λaux =0.01, λcal =0.05; ceilings\n390\tMmax =5.0, ϕmax =π/4. Full procedure in Appendix B.\n391\tWhy the components are coupled, not stacked. The central design property—a fusion that routes\n392\ton epistemic vs. aleatoric error regimes at a sampling budget derived from training—requires all\n393\tthree components and survives no proper subset. Three counterfactuals make this concrete. (C1)\n394\tSymmetric uncertainty heads on both pathways (the standard dual-branch ensemble): the fusion sees\n395\ttwo aleatoric scalars and no epistemic one, making it blind to spectral expert disagreement. Thus,\n396\tthe mixing weight collapses into a generic confidence average, consistent with the 0.17 PESQ drop\n397\tobserved for “w/o uncertainty fusion” in Figure 3a. (C2) Homogeneous MoE in place of distinct\n398\tarchetypes: top-k blends span one function class instead of distinct inductive biases. In this way,\n399\tuepi no longer indicates which bias is mismatched but only weight perturbations among identicalarchitecture experts, resulting in 0.43 PESQ drop as shown in Figure 3a. (C3) Drop the regularizers:\n400\tTheorem 1’s second term loses its training-time upper bound, so small-K inference is no longer\n401\tderivable; uale inherits the failure since the SB error it was calibrated against is now uncontrolled.\n402\tRemoving the SB pathway entirely, leading to −0.63 PESQ, is the limit of this collapse. In all three\n403\tcases the asymmetric fusion reduces to a fixed-weight or generic ensemble, the small-K guarantee\n404\tdisappears, or both: the MoE produces the error only uepi detects, the regularized SB produces the\n405\terror only uale detects, Theorem 1 controls the SB-side error at the deployment budget, and the\n406\tasymmetric fusion is the only mechanism that routes between them. The ablation pattern in Figure 3a\n407\tof Section 4 is the empirical signature of this coupling.\n408\t\n409\t4\n410\t\n411\tExperiments and Discussion\n412\t\n413\tSetup. Dataset: We evaluate our method on VoiceBank+DEMAND [50, 46], a widely used benchmark containing 11,572 training and 824 test utterances from 28 and 2 speakers, respectively. Clean\n414\tspeech is mixed with 14 noise types. All audio signals are resampled to 16 kHz. Metrics: We\n415\treport PESQ [51] and STOI [52] for perceptual quality and intelligibility, composite metrics CSIG,\n416\tCBAK, and COVL [53] for signal/background/overall quality, Expected Calibration Error (ECE) [44]\n417\tfor uncertainty calibration, and Real-Time Factor (RTF) with end-to-end latency for computational\n418\tefficiency. Implementation: We use STFT with 1024-point FFT, 256-sample hop (16 ms), and Hann\n419\twindow, yielding 513 frequency bins. The MoE comprises 5 archetype experts (one per scene family\n420\t7\n421\t\n422\t\fTable 1: Performance comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. Best results in bold (ties bolded jointly).\n423\tAll baseline numbers are reproduced by us under a unified evaluation protocol. Our method achieves\n424\tthe best score on every metric, with calibrated uncertainty estimates as an additional benefit.\n425\tMethod\n426\t\n427\tType\n428\t\n429\tPESQ↑\n430\t\n431\tSTOI↑\n432\t\n433\tCSIG↑\n434\t\n435\tCBAK↑\n436\t\n437\tCOVL↑\n438\t\n439\tNoisy\n440\t\n441\t–\n442\t\n443\t1.97\n444\t\n445\t0.91\n446\t\n447\t–\n448\t\n449\t–\n450\t\n451\t–\n452\t\n453\tDiscriminative\n454\tDiscriminative\n455\tDiscriminative\n456\t\n457\t2.16\n458\t3.55\n459\t3.73\n460\t\n461\t0.92\n462\t0.96\n463\t0.96\n464\t\n465\t–\n466\t4.79\n467\t4.82\n468\t\n469\t–\n470\t3.63\n471\t3.67\n472\t\n473\t–\n474\t4.37\n475\t4.40\n476\t\n477\tSGMSE+ [11]\n478\tSBCTM [36]\n479\tSB-SE [28]\n480\tROSE-CD [33]\n481\t\n482\tDiffusion\n483\tSB-based\n484\tSB-based\n485\tDistillation\n486\t\n487\t3.45\n488\t3.58\n489\t3.70\n490\t3.85\n491\t\n492\t0.95\n493\t0.95\n494\t0.95\n495\t0.96\n496\t\n497\t4.71\n498\t4.66\n499\t4.77\n500\t4.63\n501\t\n502\t3.64\n503\t3.43\n504\t3.75\n505\t3.37\n506\t\n507\t4.31\n508\t4.52\n509\t4.48\n510\t4.30\n511\t\n512\tHybridSB-MoE (Ours)\n513\t\n514\tHybrid\n515\t\n516\t3.88\n517\t\n518\t0.96\n519\t\n520\t4.82\n521\t\n522\t3.85\n523\t\n524\t4.82\n525\t\n526\tSEGAN [4]\n527\tSEMamba [17]\n528\tMamba-SEUNet [18]\n529\t\n530\tin VoiceBank+DEMAND, see Appendix A) with Top-k=2 routing. The waveform U-Net has 4\n531\tencoder/decoder levels with a transformer bottleneck. Training uses AdamW (learning rate 2 × 10−4 ,\n532\tcosine schedule, 200 epochs, batch size 32) on 2 NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs. Full hyperparameters\n533\tare listed in Appendix B. Baselines: We compare against discriminative methods (SEGAN [4],\n534\tSEMamba [17], Mamba-SEUNet [18]), diffusion/SB-based generative models (SGMSE+ [11], SBSE [28], SBCTM [36]), and the consistency-distilled ROSE-CD [33]. All baselines are retrained from\n535\tscratch using their official implementations under a unified protocol (matched preprocessing, 16 kHz\n536\tsampling, train/test split), and all reported metrics are computed by us with a shared evaluation script\n537\tto avoid implementation-induced discrepancies.\n538\tMain results. Table 1 reports the comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE attains\n539\tthe best score on every metric, strictly dominating on PESQ, CBAK, and COVL and tying for the\n540\tlead on STOI and CSIG. Two clarifications anchor the comparison. (i) We do not claim the smallest\n541\tsampling budget as ROSE-CD reaches a single step via consistency distillation. At our K=8 we\n542\tstrictly dominate SGMSE+, SB-SE, and SBCTM at their larger budgets, and exceed ROSE-CD across\n543\tall five quality metrics despite its smaller K. (ii) The CBAK gain is the most informative single\n544\tnumber, i.e., +0.10 over the strongest SB baseline (SB-SE) and +0.48 over ROSE-CD, indicating\n545\tthat dual-domain processing suppresses background noise more effectively than either pure-spectral\n546\tor pure-waveform generative pipelines. At the same time, the COVL gain (4.82 vs. 4.52) confirms\n547\tthis without sacrificing signal fidelity. Furthermore, the choice K=8 is not heuristic. By Theorem 1,\n548\tonce L⋆path + L⋆traj is minimized, the C1 K −α term saturates at modest K, see Appendix E.\n549\tAblation studies. Figure 3a reports the ablation results. The performance drops follow the asymmetric\n550\tuncertainty design. Removing the SB pathway causes the largest degradation (−0.63 PESQ), since the\n551\tmodel loses both waveform-domain phase coherence and the aleatoric signal needed for asymmetric\n552\tfusion. Removing the MoE module yields a smaller but still substantial drop (−0.43 PESQ), as the\n553\tSB pathway remains active but the complementary epistemic signal from expert disagreement is lost.\n554\tReplacing uncertainty-aware fusion with equal weighting also reduces performance (−0.17 PESQ),\n555\tshowing that both pathways remain useful but require principled routing rather than generic averaging.\n556\tThis ordering, 0.63 > 0.43 > 0.17, supports our central design claim: ablations that remove a\n557\tdistinct uncertainty channel are more damaging than those that only weaken fusion over intact\n558\tpathways. Sequential variants in either direction also underperform parallel fusion (−0.30/−0.39\n559\tPESQ), suggesting that cascading one domain through the other weakens the structural source of\n560\tpathway-specific uncertainty. Figure 3b further validates the sparse routing choice. Increasing k\n561\tfrom 1 to 2 improves PESQ from 3.74 to 3.88 with a modest RTF of 0.28, indicating that two active\n562\texperts provide useful complementary inductive biases. Increasing to k=3 adds only +0.01 PESQ\n563\twhile increasing RTF by 25%, so we adopt top-k=2 routing as the default quality–latency trade-off.\n564\tEfficiency and robustness. Figures 3c and 3d report efficiency and robustness. With only 8 sampling\n565\tsteps, HybridSB-MoE achieves an RTF of 0.28 (35 ms latency), giving a 4–5× speedup over SGMSE+\n566\tand SB-SE while surpassing their PESQ; together with ROSE-CD, our method defines the Pareto\n567\tfrontier with a markedly higher quality ceiling. The dual-domain design is most beneficial at low\n568\tSNR (+0.13 over ROSE-CD at 0 dB). Beyond quality, the calibration loss of Eq. (11) yields a fusionweight ECE of 0.042, an order-of-magnitude reduction from the 0.12 achieved by an uncalibrated\n569\tsingle-pathway baseline; this is what makes uepi and uale usable as confidence signals for downstream\n570\t8\n571\t\n572\t\f3.88\n573\t\n574\t4.0\n575\t\n576\tw/o uncertainty fusion 68M\n577\t\n578\t3.71 (-0.17)\n579\t\n580\t3.9\n581\t\n582\t3.49 (-0.39)\n583\t\n584\tSeq. (MoE SB) 65M\n585\t\n586\t3.25 (-0.63)\n587\t\n588\tw/o SB pathway 41M\n589\t3.2\n590\t\n591\t3.4\n592\t\n593\t3.8\n594\t3.7\n595\t3.6\n596\t\n597\t3.45 (-0.43)\n598\t\n599\tw/o MoE (single expert) 23M\n600\t\n601\t3.0\n602\t\n603\tPESQ\n604\t\n605\t3.58 (-0.30)\n606\t\n607\tSeq. (SB MoE) 65M\n608\t\n609\t3.5\n610\t3.6\n611\t\n612\tPESQ\n613\t\n614\t3.8\n615\t\n616\tPESQ\n617\tRTF\n618\t\n619\t4.0\n620\t\n621\t3.62\n622\t\n623\t0.19\n624\t\n625\t3 exp.\n626\tk=1\n627\t\n628\tROSE-CD\n629\t\n630\tSB-SE\n631\t\n632\t3.7\n633\t3.6\n634\t3.5\n635\t3.4\n636\t\n637\tParams\n638\t\n639\t58M\n640\t\n641\t0.05\n642\t\n643\t63M\n644\t\n645\t0.1\n646\t\n647\t0.2\n648\t\n649\tSBCTM\n650\t68M\n651\t\n652\t0.5\n653\t\n654\tPESQ\n655\t\n656\tPESQ\n657\t\n658\t3.8\n659\t\n660\tReal-time\n661\tboundary\n662\t\n663\tOurs\n664\t\n665\t0.5\n666\t\n667\t3.89\n668\t\n669\t3.88\n670\t3.74\n671\t\n672\t0.21\n673\t\n674\t0.28\n675\t\n676\t0.35\n677\t\n678\t0.4\n679\t0.3\n680\t0.2\n681\t0.1\n682\t\n683\t5 exp.\n684\tk=1\n685\t\n686\t5 exp.\n687\tk=2\n688\t\n689\tExpert count and top-k\n690\t\n691\t5 exp.\n692\tk=3\n693\t\n694\t0.0\n695\t\n696\t(b) Expert count, k\n697\t\n698\t(a) Components\n699\t4.0 Best quality efficiency\n700\ttrade-off\n701\t3.9\n702\t\n703\tDefault\n704\t(best trade-off)\n705\t\n706\tRTF\n707\t\n708\tFull model 68M\n709\t\n710\tSGMSE+\n711\t1.0\n712\t\n713\tReal-Time Factor (RTF, log scale)\n714\t\n715\t2.0\n716\t\n717\t(c) Quality–RTF\n718\t\n719\t4.75\n720\t4.50\n721\t4.25\n722\t4.00\n723\t3.75\n724\t3.50\n725\t3.25\n726\t3.00\n727\t2.75\n728\t\n729\t4.48\n730\t4.12\n731\t3.79\n732\t3.31\n733\tSEMamba\n734\tROSE-CD\n735\tOurs\n736\t0 dB\n737\t\n738\t5 dB\n739\t\n740\t10 dB\n741\t\n742\tInput SNR (dB)\n743\t\n744\t15 dB\n745\t\n746\t(d) PESQ vs. SNR\n747\t\n748\tFigure 3: Ablation studies and efficiency analysis. (a) Each architectural component contributes\n749\tindependently. (b) Top-k=2 with 5 experts hits the quality–efficiency sweet spot. (c) Marker size ∝\n750\tparameter count; dashed line is RTF=1.0. (d) PESQ across SNR levels.\n751\tdecisions, not just as training-time auxiliaries. A scene-stratified breakdown across all 14 noise\n752\ttypes appears in Appendix D, with PESQ standard deviation < 0.03 confirming that combinatorial\n753\tarchetype coverage generalizes across scenes without per-noise tuning.\n754\tScope and limitations. We evaluate on VoiceBank+DEMAND, the standard SE benchmark; sceneadaptivity claims should be retested on broader corpora (DNS, WHAMR!, CHiME) where train and\n755\ttest noise distributions diverge more substantially. Theorem 1 is a design-justifying inequality with\n756\tworst-case constants C1 , C2 ; quantitatively fitting the predicted K −α rate against finer NFE sweeps is\n757\ta natural follow-up. The framework is single-channel; multi-channel extensions are direct (additional\n758\tinput streams to both pathways) but beyond this paper’s scope. Finally, while the heterogeneous\n759\tarchetype set generalizes by adding archetypes per new noise family (Appendix A), automatic\n760\tarchetype discovery from data, rather than expert-designed primitives, remains open.\n761\t\n762\t5\n763\t\n764\tConclusion\n765\t\n766\tCombining a Schrödinger Bridge with a heterogeneous mixture-of-experts is natural; the key question\n767\tis how to make the combination synergistic. We do so through two linked ideas. First, pathwaytyped asymmetric uncertainty fusion uses each pathway’s characteristic uncertainty signal, epistemic\n768\tdisagreement from the spectral MoE and aleatoric variance from the waveform SB, so the mixing\n769\tweight selects between error regimes rather than averages predictions. Second, a discretization\n770\tbound (Theorem 1) links the small-K inference budget to two explicit training regularizers, replacing\n771\theuristic step-count claims with a provable objective-level guarantee. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, this\n772\tco-design HybridSB-MoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at matched step budgets\n773\twhile remaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods. Three counterfactual\n774\tablations show that no proper subset of components preserves the central design: the asymmetric\n775\tfusion needs both a multi-expert path (for epistemic disagreement) and a stochastic bridge (for\n776\taleatoric variance), and the small-K guarantee needs both regularizers. For SE, this suggests future\n777\tdual-domain systems should be organized by the type of error each pathway faces, not just predictive\n778\tcomplementarity. For generative modelling, the discretization bound shows that few-step inference\n779\tcan be derived from training if regularizers are chosen to control the two sources of K-step error.\n780\tFuture work will extend the framework to multi-channel input, larger benchmarks (DNS, WHAMR!,\n781\tCHiME), and finer NFE sweeps to empirically fit the predicted K −α rate.\n782\t\n783\t9\n784\t\n785\t\fReferences\n786\t[1] D. Wang and J. Chen, “Supervised speech separation based on deep learning: An overview,”\n787\tIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 26, no. 10, pp.\n788\t1702–1726, 2018.\n789\t[2] A. Defossez, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “Real time speech enhancement in the waveform domain,”\n790\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2006.12847, 2020.\n791\t[3] Y. Xu, J. Du, L.-R. Dai, and C.-H. Lee, “A regression approach to speech enhancement based on\n792\tdeep neural networks,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing,\n793\tvol. 23, no. 1, pp. 7–19, 2015.\n794\t[4] S. Pascual, A. Bonafonte, and J. Serra, “Segan: Speech enhancement generative adversarial\n795\tnetwork,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1703.09452, 2017.\n796\t[5] Y. Luo and N. Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal time–frequency magnitude masking\n797\tfor speech separation,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech and Lang. Proc., vol. 27, no. 8, p.\n798\t1256–1266, Aug. 2019. [Online]. Available: https://doi.org/10.1109/TASLP.2019.2915167\n799\t[6] Y. Luo, Z. Chen, and T. Yoshioka, “Dual-path rnn: efficient long sequence modeling for\n800\ttime-domain single-channel speech separation,” in ICASSP, 2020, pp. 46–50.\n801\t[7] J. Chen, Z. Wang, D. Tuo, Z. Wu, S. Kang, and H. Meng, “Fullsubnet+: Channel attention\n802\tfullsubnet with complex spectrograms for speech enhancement,