om, H. Erdogan, and J. R. Hershey, “Sdr – half-baked or well done?” in\n827\tICASSP 2019 - 2019 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n828\t(ICASSP), 2019, pp. 626–630.\n829\t[17] R. Chao, W.-H. Cheng, M. La Quatra, S. M. Siniscalchi, C.-H. H. Yang, S.-W. Fu, and Y. Tsao,\n830\t“An investigation of incorporating mamba for speech enhancement,” in 2024 IEEE Spoken\n831\tLanguage Technology Workshop (SLT). IEEE, 2024, pp. 302–308.\n832\t10\n833\t\n834\t\f[18] J. Wang, Z. Lin, T. Wang, M. Ge, L. Wang, and J. Dang, “Mamba-seunet: Mamba unet for\n835\tmonaural speech enhancement,” in ICASSP 2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on\n836\tAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2025, pp. 1–5.\n837\t[19] H. Shi, K. Shimada, M. Hirano, T. Shibuya, Y. Koyama, Z. Zhong, S. Takahashi, T. Kawahara,\n838\tand Y. Mitsufuji, “Diffusion-based speech enhancement with joint generative and predictive\n839\tdecoders,” in ICASSP 2024 - 2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and\n840\tSignal Processing (ICASSP), 2024, pp. 12 951–12 955.\n841\t[20] S. T. Yousif and B. M. Mahmmod, “Speech enhancement algorithms: A systematic literature\n842\treview,” Algorithms, vol. 18, no. 5, p. 272, 2025.\n843\t[21] T. Chen, G.-H. Liu, and E. A. Theodorou, “Likelihood training of schrödinger bridge using\n844\tforward–backward sdes theory,” in ICLR, 2022.\n845\t[22] Y. Song, J. Sohl-Dickstein, D. P. Kingma, A. Kumar, S. Ermon, and B. Poole, “Score-based\n846\tgenerative modeling through stochastic differential equations,” in International Conference\n847\ton Learning Representations, 2021. [Online]. Available: https://openreview.net/forum?id=\n848\tPxTIG12RRHS\n849\t[23] J. Song, C. Meng, and S. Ermon, “Denoising diffusion implicit models,” arXiv preprint\n850\tarXiv:2010.02502, 2020.\n851\t[24] Y. Lipman, R. T. Q. Chen, H. Ben-Hamu, M. Nickel, and M. Le, “Flow matching for generative\n852\tmodeling,” in International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n853\t[25] X. Liu, C. Gong, and Q. Liu, “Flow straight and fast: Learning to generate and transfer data\n854\twith rectified flow,” in International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n855\t[26] V. De Bortoli, J. Thornton, J. Heng, and A. Doucet, “Diffusion Schrödinger bridge with\n856\tapplications to score-based generative modeling,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing\n857\tSystems, vol. 34, 2021, pp. 17 695–17 709.\n858\t[27] Y. Shi, V. De Bortoli, A. Campbell, and A. Doucet, “Diffusion schrödinger bridge matching,”\n859\tin Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, A. Oh, T. Naumann, A. Globerson,\n860\tK. Saenko, M. Hardt, and S. Levine, Eds., vol. 36. Curran Associates, Inc., 2023, pp.\n861\t62 183–62 223. [Online]. Available: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/\n862\tc428adf74782c2092d254329b6b02482-Paper-Conference.pdf\n863\t[28] A. Jukić, R. Korostik, J. Balam, and B. Ginsburg, “Schrödinger bridge for generative speech\n864\tenhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.16074, 2024.\n865\t[29] S. Wang, S. Liu, A. Harper, P. Kendrick, M. Salzmann, and M. Cernak, “Diffusion-based\n866\tspeech enhancement with Schrödinger bridge and symmetric noise schedule,” arXiv preprint\n867\tarXiv:2409.05116, 2024.\n868\t[30] Z. Tang, T. Hang, S. Gu, D. Chen, and B. Guo, “Simplified diffusion schrödinger bridge,” arXiv\n869\tpreprint arXiv:2403.14623, 2024.\n870\t[31] J. Yang, S. Wang, C. Wu, L. Guo, and F. Fan, “Schrödinger bridge mamba for one-step speech\n871\tenhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.16834, 2025.\n872\t[32] H. Zhang, G. Li, P. Wu, Y. Gao, and H. Zhang, “Sb-senet: Diffusion model based on schrödinger\n873\tbridge for speech enhancement,” Applied Acoustics, vol. 236, p. 110742, 2025.\n874\t[33] L. Xu, L. F. Yan, and W. B. Kleijn, “Robust one-step speech enhancement via consistency\n875\tdistillation,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.05688, 2025.\n876\t[34] Y. Song, P. Dhariwal, M. Chen, and I. Sutskever, “Consistency models,” in International\n877\tConference on Machine Learning, 2023.\n878\t[35] S. Han, S. Lee, J. Lee, and K. Lee, “Few-step adversarial schrödinger bridge for generative\n879\tspeech enhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.01460, 2025.\n880\t11\n881\t\n882\t\f[36] S. Nishigori, K. Saito, N. Murata, M. Hirano, S. Takahashi, and Y. Mitsufuji,\n883\t“Schrödinger bridge consistency trajectory models for speech enhancement,” arXiv preprint\n884\tarXiv:2507.11925, 2025.\n885\t[37] S. Chen, S. Chewi, J. Li, Y. Li, A. Salim, and A. R. Zhang, “Sampling is as easy as learning the\n886\tscore: theory for diffusion models with minimal data assumptions,” in International Conference\n887\ton Learning Representations, 2023.\n888\t[38] N. Shazeer, A. Mirhoseini, K. Maziarz, A. Davis, Q. Le, G. Hinton, and J. Dean, “Outrageously large neural networks: The sparsely-gated mixture-of-experts layer,” arXiv preprint\n889\tarXiv:1701.06538, 2017.\n890\t[39] W. Fedus, B. Zoph, and N. Shazeer, “Switch transformers: Scaling to trillion parameter models\n891\twith simple and efficient sparsity,” Journal of Machine Learning Research, vol. 23, no. 120, pp.\n892\t1–39, 2022. [Online]. Available: http://jmlr.org/papers/v23/21-0998.html\n893\t[40] D. Lepikhin, H. Lee, Y. Xu, D. Chen, O. Firat, Y. Huang, M. Krikun, N. Shazeer, and Z. Chen,\n894\t“Gshard: Scaling giant models with conditional computation and automatic sharding,” arXiv\n895\tpreprint arXiv:2006.16668, 2020.\n896\t[41] A. Sivaraman and M. Kim, “Zero-shot personalized speech enhancement through speakerinformed model selection,” in 2021 IEEE Workshop on Applications of Signal Processing to\n897\tAudio and Acoustics (WASPAA), 2021, pp. 171–175.\n898\t[42] R. Miccini, M. Kim, C. Laroche, L. Pezzarossa, and P. Smaragdis, “Adaptive slimming for\n899\tscalable and efficient speech enhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.04879, 2025.\n900\t[43] B. Lakshminarayanan, A. Pritzel, and C. Blundell, “Simple and scalable predictive uncertainty\n901\testimation using deep ensembles,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems,\n902\tvol. 30, 2017.\n903\t[44] C. Guo, G. Pleiss, Y. Sun, and K. Q. Weinberger, “On calibration of modern neural networks,”\n904\tin Proceedings of the 34th International Conference on Machine Learning - Volume 70, ser.\n905\tICML’17. JMLR.org, 2017, p. 1321–1330.\n906\t[45] J. B. Allen and L. R. Rabiner, “A unified approach to short-time fourier analysis and synthesis,”\n907\tProceedings of the IEEE, vol. 65, no. 11, pp. 1558–1564, 1977.\n908\t[46] J. Thiemann, N. Ito, and E. Vincent, “The diverse environments multi-channel acoustic\n909\tnoise database (demand): A database of multichannel environmental noise recordings,”\n910\tProceedings of Meetings on Acoustics, vol. 19, no. 1, p. 035081, 05 2013. [Online]. Available:\n911\thttps://doi.org/10.1121/1.4799597\n912\t[47] Y. Wu and K. He, “Group normalization,” in Proceedings of the European Conference on\n913\tComputer Vision, 2018, pp. 3–19.\n914\t[48] G. Peyre and M. Cuturi, “Computational optimal transport,” Foundations and Trends in Machine\n915\tLearning, vol. 11, no. 5-6, pp. 355–607, 2019.\n916\t[49] A. Nichol and P. Dhariwal, “Improved denoising diffusion probabilistic models,” 2021.\n917\t[Online]. Available: https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09672\n918\t[50] C. Valentini-Botinhao, X. Wang, S. Takaki, and J. Yamagishi, “Investigating rnn-based speech\n919\tenhancement methods for noise-robust text-to-speech,” in 9th ISCA Workshop on Speech\n920\tSynthesis Workshop (SSW 9), 2016, pp. 146–152.\n921\t[51] A. Rix, J. Beerends, M. Hollier, and A. Hekstra, “Perceptual evaluation of speech quality\n922\t(pesq)-a new method for speech quality assessment of telephone networks and codecs,” in 2001\n923\tIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing. Proceedings (Cat.\n924\tNo.01CH37221), vol. 2, 2001, pp. 749–752 vol.2.\n925\t[52] C. H. Taal, R. C. Hendriks, R. Heusdens, and J. Jensen, “An algorithm for intelligibility\n926\tprediction of time–frequency weighted noisy speech,” IEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech,\n927\tand Language Processing, vol. 19, no. 7, pp. 2125–2136, 2011.\n928\t12\n929\t\n930\t\f[53] Y. Hu and P. C. Loizou, “Evaluation of objective quality measures for speech enhancement,”\n931\tIEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 16, no. 1, pp. 229–238,\n932\t2008.\n933\t[54] N. Tishby and N. Zaslavsky, “Deep learning and the information bottleneck principle,” in 2015\n934\tIEEE Information Theory Workshop (ITW), 2015, pp. 1–5.\n935\t\n936\t13\n937\t\n938\t\fA\n939\t\n940\tHeterogeneous Expert Architectures\n941\t\n942\tThis appendix details the heterogeneous MoE layer described in Section 3 of the main paper. We\n943\tdescribe the design philosophy that connects each architectural archetype to a canonical noise family,\n944\tlist the configurations used for VoiceBank+DEMAND, and discuss how the archetype set generalizes\n945\tto other datasets.\n946\tA.1\n947\t\n948\tDesign Philosophy\n949\t\n950\tThe heterogeneous MoE is grounded in the empirical observation that real-world acoustic noise,\n951\tdespite its surface diversity, can be decomposed into a small number of canonical processing primitives. Stationary tonal noise (e.g., kitchen appliances, refrigerator hum) is well-modeled by compact\n952\tnormalized layers with low-rank structure; ambient natural backgrounds (e.g., park, river, field)\n953\tbenefit from wide receptive fields with strong normalization to capture diffuse spectral content; office\n954\tand meeting environments contain overlapping voices and structured interference best handled by\n955\tinformation bottlenecks [54]; transportation noise (bus, car, metro) exhibits strong harmonic and\n956\tquasi-periodic energy from engines and motors, naturally captured by harmonic basis expansion; and\n957\tunstructured public-space mixtures (cafeteria, station, restaurant) require universal-approximationstyle general-purpose blocks. Rather than scaling capacity by replicating one architecture (as in\n958\thomogeneous MoE), we instantiate one expert per archetype, then rely on sparse routing to combine\n959\tthem per input.\n960\tFigure 4 (reproduced here for completeness) illustrates the shared backbone and the architecturally\n961\tdistinct instantiations. All experts pass features through LayerNorm → variable internal modules\n962\t→ bottleneck projection, ensuring interface compatibility for routing while permitting structurally\n963\tdivergent computation in the middle.\n964\t\n965\tFigure 4: Heterogeneous expert architectures (the N =5 archetypes used for VoiceBank+DEMAND).\n966\tLeft: shared backbone with variable module slots. Right: example instantiations with architecturally distinct modules, each targeting a different noise-processing primitive (Home, Nature, Office,\n967\tTransport, Public). Built on this\u0001 compact set of canonical archetypes, sparse top-k=2 routing blends\n968\texperts per input to span the 52 =10-dimensional simplex of pairwise mixtures, yielding combinatorial coverage of the 14 noise types in VoiceBank+DEMAND without requiring a dedicated expert per\n969\tenvironment.\n970\t\n971\t14\n972\t\n973\t\fTable 2: Heterogeneous expert architectures used for VoiceBank+DEMAND. The notation GN(g)\n974\tdenotes group normalization with g groups; LN denotes layer normalization. Each architecture is\n975\tselected to match the inductive bias of its target scene family.\n976\tScene Category\n977\tHome (DKITCHEN, etc.)\n978\tNature (NPARK, etc.)\n979\tOffice (OMEETING, etc.)\n980\tTransport (TBUS, etc.)\n981\tPublic (PCAFETER, etc.)\n982\t\n983\tA.2\n984\t\n985\tArchitecture\n986\t513 → 1024 → GN(8) → 1024 → 513\n987\t513 → 2048 → 1024 → LN → 513\n988\t513 → 1024 → 512 → 1024 → 513\n989\t513 → 1536 → 1024 → 513\n990\t513 → 1024 → LN → 1024 → 513\n991\t\n992\tDesign Motivation\n993\tLow-rank denoising for stationary tonal noise\n994\tWide receptive field for diffuse ambience\n995\tInformation bottleneck [54]\n996\tHarmonic basis expansion\n997\tGeneral-purpose universal approximator\n998\t\n999\tParams\n1000\t2.6M\n1001\t3.7M\n1002\t2.6M\n1003\t3.2M\n1004\t2.6M\n1005\t\n1006\tExpert Configurations for VoiceBank+DEMAND\n1007\t\n1008\tTable 2 lists the architectural specifications used in our VoiceBank+DEMAND experiments. Each\n1009\tarchetype maps to one of the major noise families in the dataset; we instantiate one expert per\n1010\tarchetype, yielding a compact yet diverse set of N = 5 processing primitives. The input dimension\n1011\tmatches the STFT frequency-bin count F = 513, and all experts produce outputs of the same\n1012\tdimension to maintain interface compatibility for routing.\n1013\tA.3\n1014\t\n1015\tArchetype Details\n1016\t\n1017\tHome expert. The kitchen, washing-machine, and living-room scenes share a common acoustic\n1018\tsignature: stationary tonal noise from appliances overlaid on relatively clean speech. We instantiate\n1019\ta compact two-layer expansion (513 → 1024) followed by group normalization with 8 groups,\n1020\twhich acts as a low-rank denoising operator. The narrow internal capacity is sufficient because the\n1021\tnoise subspace is approximately stationary, allowing the expert to suppress predictable tonal patterns\n1022\twithout overfitting.\n1023\tNature expert. Park, river, and field recordings contain diffuse, broadband ambient energy that fills\n1024\twide spectral regions. We employ a wider hidden layer (513 → 2048 → 1024) followed by layer\n1025\tnormalization to maintain stable activations across the expanded receptive field. This architecture\n1026\ttrades parameter count for the spectral coverage required to model ambient acoustic textures.\n1027\tOffice expert. Meeting and office scenes feature overlapping voices and structured background\n1028\tchatter. We adopt an information-bottleneck design [54] (513 → 1024 → 512 → 1024 → 513) that\n1029\texplicitly compresses to a 512-dimensional representation in the middle, encouraging the expert to\n1030\tretain only task-relevant information and suppress competing voiced sources.\n1031\tTransport expert. Bus, car, and metro noise is dominated by harmonic and quasi-periodic energy\n1032\tfrom engines, motors, and wheel-rail interactions. We use a wide-narrow-output structure (513 →\n1033\t1536 → 1024 → 513) without intermediate normalization, allowing the expert to fit a learned\n1034\tharmonic basis at the first layer and refine it before projection. Skipping internal normalization\n1035\tpreserves amplitude information critical for engine-noise spectral envelopes.\n1036\tPublic expert. Cafeteria, station, and restaurant scenes are the most challenging, combining nonstationary crowd noise, music, transient events, and reverberation. We adopt a symmetric architecture\n1037\twith a single LayerNorm in the middle (513 → 1024 → LN → 1024 → 513), which serves as a\n1038\tuniversal approximator for the heterogeneous mixture without strong inductive bias. Sparse routing\n1039\tallows this general-purpose expert to be combined with archetype-specific experts when partial\n1040\tstructure is detectable in the input.\n1041\tA.4\n1042\t\n1043\tGeneralization to Other Datasets\n1044\t\n1045\tThe archetype set is intentionally modular: adding a new expert requires only conforming to the\n1046\tshared backbone interface (matching input/output dimensionality F ). The auxiliary load-balancing\n1047\tloss (Eq. 2) automatically integrates new experts into the routing distribution without retraining the\n1048\texisting ones from scratch, with brief router fine-tuning recommended. For deployments encountering\n1049\tnoise families not represented in the current set, for instance, reverberant cocktail-party recordings or\n1050\tindustrial machinery noise, a corresponding archetype can be added (e.g., a dereverberation-oriented\n1051\texpert with longer temporal context) without altering the rest of the framework.\n1052\t15\n1053\t\n1054\t\fTable 3: Loss weights and bound parameters used in all VoiceBank+DEMAND experiments.\n1055\tHyperparameter\n1056\t\n1057\tValue\n1058\t\n1059\tHyperparameter\n1060\t\n1061\tValue\n1062\t\n1063\tλSB (overall SB)\n1064\tλpath (path consistency)\n1065\tλtraj (trajectory)\n1066\tMmax (mask ceiling)\n1067\tϕmax (phase ceiling)\n1068\t\n1069\t1.0\n1070\t0.1\n1071\t0.05\n1072\t5.0\n1073\tπ/4\n1074\t\n1075\tλaux (load balance)\n1076\tλcal (calibration)\n1077\tλI (importance variance)\n1078\tλL (load variance)\n1079\t—\n1080\t\n1081\t0.01\n1082\t0.05\n1083\t0.5\n1084\t0.5\n1085\t—\n1086\t\n1087\tB\n1088\t\n1089\tU-Net and Training Details\n1090\t\n1091\tB.1\n1092\t\n1093\tWaveform-Domain U-Net Architecture\n1094\t\n1095\tThe denoiser x̂θ is a 1D U-Net with 4 encoder/decoder levels. Each encoder block applies a strided\n1096\t1D convolution (stride 2, kernel 7) followed by GroupNorm and SiLU activation, halving temporal\n1097\tresolution and doubling channels (64 → 128 → 256 → 512). The decoder mirrors this structure with\n1098\ttransposed convolutions and skip connections. A latent bottleneck operates at the lowest resolution to\n1099\taggregate global temporal context before decoding. The noisy observation y is concatenated with\n1100\txt along the channel dimension at the input. Timestep t is encoded via a sinusoidal embedding\n1101\t(dim 128) projected through a 2-layer MLP (256 → 512), and injected into every block via FiLM\n1102\tscale-and-shift conditioning. The aleatoric uncertainty head σθ2 is a separate 2-layer MLP attached to\n1103\tthe final decoder feature, producing per-sample log-variance.\n1104\tB.2\n1105\t\n1106\tLoss Weights\n1107\t\n1108\tThe full training objective combines six loss components (Lrec , Ldata\n1109\tSB , Lpath , Ltraj , Laux , Lcal )\n1110\twith the following weights, tuned via grid search on a 10% held-out validation split of VoiceBank+DEMAND, as shown in Table 3.\n1111\tB.3\n1112\t\n1113\tTraining Schedule\n1114\t\n1115\tWe use AdamW with β1 = 0.9, β2 = 0.999, weight decay 0.01, and gradient clipping at norm 1.0.\n1116\tThe learning rate follows cosine annealing from 2 × 10−4 to 1 × 10−6 over 200 epochs, with a\n1117\t5-epoch linear warm-up. Batch size is 32 across 2 NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (effective batch 64).\n1118\tMixed-precision (bfloat16) training is used throughout. Total wall-clock training time: approximately\n1119\t48 hours.\n1120\tB.4\n1121\t\n1122\tSB Sampling Configuration\n1123\t\n1124\t\u0001\n1125\tThe cumulative cosine schedule follows β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 (t/T + s)/(1 + s) · π/2\n1126\tand offset s = 0.008, yielding the per-step ratio βt = β̄t /β̄t−1 . We adopt the convention that\n1127\tβ̄t decreases monotonically from β̄0 = 1 to β̄T = 0, which keeps Eq. 4 symmetric in x and y;\n1128\tthis assigns β̄t the role played by ᾱt in the standard DDPM convention. The bridge perturbation\n1129\tin both thep\n1130\tforward construction (Eq. 4) and the reverse update (Eq. 5) shares a single schedule\n1131\tσt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t ) with σmax = 0.05, which vanishes at both endpoints (σ0 = σT = 0) and\n1132\tmatches the boundary conditions x0 = x, xT = y. For inference, we use K = 8 sampling steps with\n1133\tthe non-uniform schedule tk = T (k/K)γ where γ = 0.6 (front-loaded).\n1134\tAt each training step we sample a single timestep t ∼ U [0, T ] and supervise x̂θ via Eq. 6, while\n1135\tLpath (Eq. 7) is evaluated on an independently sampled pair (t, t′ ) with independent perturbations\n1136\tϵ, ϵ′ drawn from Eq. 4. Although inference uses a K-step rollout while training optimizes singlestep predictions, Lpath explicitly enforces that x̂θ (xt , y, t) remains consistent across timesteps, so\n1137\tthe multi-step trajectory operates on predictions that the model has been trained to keep mutually\n1138\tagreeable. The trajectory regularizer Ltraj further anchors each per-step prediction to the forward\n1139\tbridge construction, yielding a reverse process that is stable under the discretization mismatch\n1140\tbetween training and inference.\n1141\t16\n1142\t\n1143\t\fB.5\n1144\t\n1145\tAlgorithm Details\n1146\t\n1147\tThe complete training procedure with explicit loss accumulation is given in Algorithm 1.\n1148\tAlgorithm 1 HybridSB-MoE end-to-end training (full version)\n1149\tN\n1150\tInput: Paired training set {(y (i) , x(i) )}M\n1151\ti=1 ; experts {Ei }i=1 ; router G; SB denoiser x̂θ ; fusion\n1152\tnetwork F ; loss weights {λ∗ }\n1153\tOutput: Trained parameters Θ\n1154\t1: for each minibatch B = {(y, x)} do\n1155\t2:\n1156\t// Spectral pathway\n1157\t3:\n1158\tCompute STFT: S{y} = STFT(y); extract z = log |S{y}|\n1159\t4:\n1160\tRoute: G(z) ← α Garch\n1161\tP (z) + (1 − α) Gtoken (z); select top-k indices Ik\n1162\t5:\n1163\tAggregate: x̂spec ← i∈Ik Gi (z) Ei (z)\n1164\t6:\n1165\tPredict M̂ , ∆ϕ via Eq. 3; reconstruct xspec via iSTFT\n1166\t7:\n1167\tCompute uepi (variance of top-k expert outputs)\n1168\t8:\n1169\t// Waveform pathway\n1170\tSample two independent timesteps t, t′ ∼ U [0, T ] and noises ϵ, ϵ′ ∼ N (0, I) // single Monte\n1171\t9:\n1172\tCarlo sample of Et,t′ per minibatch\n1173\tp\n1174\tp\n1175\t10:\n1176\tForm bridge states: xt ← p β̄t x + p1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ;\n1177\txt′ ← β̄t′ x + 1 − β̄t′ y + σt′ ϵ′\n1178\t11:\n1179\tPredict x̂θ (xt , y, t), x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ ), and aleatoric variance σθ2 at (xt , t)\n1180\t′ 2\n1181\tCompute Ldata\n1182\t12:\n1183\tSB (Eq. 6, on (xt , t)); Lpath = ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t )∥ (Eq. 7); Ltraj (Eq.\n1184\t8, on (xt , t))\n1185\t13:\n1186\tSet uale ← exp(σθ2 )t ; set xwave ← x̂θ (xt , y, t) as the current single-step prediction (full\n1187\tK-step rollout used at inference)\n1188\t14:\n1189\t// Fusion\n1190\t15:\n1191\tũepi , ũale ← z-norm(uepi , uale ) // running statistics\n1192\t16:\n1193\tw ← σ(MLP(ũepi , ũale ))\n1194\t17:\n1195\tx̂ ← w · xspec + (1 − w) · xwave\n1196\t18:\n1197\tCompute Lrec = ∥x̂ − x∥22 , Laux (Eq. 2), Lcal (Eq. 11) // Lcal uses pre-norm uepi , uale\n1198\t19:\n1199\t// Joint update\n1200\t20:\n1201\tLSB ← Ldata\n1202\tSB + λpath Lpath + λtraj Ltraj\n1203\t21:\n1204\tL ← Lrec + λSB LSB + λaux Laux + λcal Lcal\n1205\t22:\n1206\tUpdate Θ ← Θ − η∇Θ L\n1207\t23: end for\n1208\t\n1209\tC\n1210\t\n1211\tProof of Theorem 1\n1212\t\n1213\tWe prove the K-step discretization bound stated in Section 3 (Theorem 1) of the main paper. The\n1214\tstrategy is to decompose the W2 distance into (i) a one-step model-fidelity error driven by L⋆path and\n1215\tL⋆traj , and (ii) a Riemann-sum discretization error in K controlled by the schedule’s front-loading\n1216\texponent γ, then to combine the two via a synchronous coupling and a triangle inequality. The\n1217\targument adapts standard SDE-discretization analysis (cf. [37, 26]) to the doubly-conditioned bridge\n1218\tprocess of Eq. (4–5).\n1219\tC.1\n1220\t\n1221\tSetup and Notation\n1222\t\n1223\tFix a noisy observation y and let {xt }t∈[0,T ] be the true continuous-time bridge process from\n1224\tclean speech x to y, so that pbr\n1225\tt (· | y) is its time-t marginal. Let x̂θ be the trained denoiser,\n1226\tand let p̂K be the law at t=0 of the K-step rollout produced by Eq. (5) initialized at xT = y\n1227\tγ\n1228\talong the non-uniform schedule\n1229\t{tk }K\n1230\tk=0 with tK = T and tk = T (k/K) , γ ∈ (0, 1]. We use\n1231\tR\n1232\t2\n1233\t2\n1234\tW2 (µ, ν) = inf π∈Γ(µ,ν) ∥u − v∥ dπ(u, v) in the standard definition.\n1235\tThroughout, C denotes a generic constant depending only on the regularity constants of Assumption\n1236\t1; its value may change line by line.\n1237\t17\n1238\t\n1239\t\fC.2\n1240\t\n1241\tOne-step Model-fidelity Lemma\n1242\t\n1243\tLemma 1 (Model-fidelity error). Under Assumption 1, for each step tk → tk−1 of Eq. (5), the\n1244\tconditional one-step error\n1245\th\n1246\ti\n1247\t2\n1248\tδk := E x̂θ (xtk , y, tk ) − x\n1249\txtk , y\n1250\tsatisfies E[δk ] ≤ C (L⋆path + L⋆traj ) uniformly in k, where the expectation on the right is over the\n1251\tbridge construction Eq. (4).\n1252\tProof. The trajectory regularizer Ltraj of Eq. (8), evaluated at the optimum, satisfies\n1253\t\u0015\n1254\t\u0014\n1255\tq\n1256\tq\n1257\t2\n1258\t= L⋆traj .\n1259\tE xt − ( β̄t x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t y)\n1260\tp\n1261\tp\n1262\tSubstituting the forward construction xt = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ from Eq. (4) and rearranging,\n1263\tq\n1264\t\u0002\n1265\t\u0003\n1266\tβ̄t E ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x∥2 − 2 β̄t σt E[⟨x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x, ϵ⟩] + σt2 E∥ϵ∥2 = L⋆traj .\n1267\tBy\n1268\tand E∥ϵ∥2 = d (the signal dimension), the cross term is bounded by\n1269\tp\n1270\t√ Cauchy–Schwarz\n1271\t2\n1272\t2 dσt E∥x̂θ − x∥ , and bounded marginals (Assumption 1(iii)) yield\n1273\t\u0002\n1274\t\u0003\n1275\t\u0001\n1276\t1 ⋆\n1277\t2\n1278\tE ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x∥2 ≤\n1279\tLtraj + Cσt2 ≤ C(L⋆traj + σmax\n1280\t),\n1281\tβ̄t\n1282\t\n1283\t(13)\n1284\t\n1285\ton [ϵ0 , T − ϵ0 ] for any fixed ϵ0 > 0, since β̄t is bounded away from zero on this set; near the\n1286\tboundaries, the schedule’s vanishing σt controls the residual.\n1287\tThe path-consistency loss Lpath of Eq. (7) controls the deviation of x̂θ across timesteps:\n1288\t\u0002\n1289\t\u0003\n1290\tE ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ )∥2 ≤ 2L⋆path .\n1291\t\n1292\t(14)\n1293\t\n1294\tCombining Eq. (13) with Eq. (14) and the triangle inequality on the implied error gives the claimed\n1295\tbound on E[δk ].\n1296\tC.3\n1297\t\n1298\tDiscretization Lemma\n1299\t\n1300\tp\n1301\tp\n1302\tLemma 2 (Riemann discretization rate). Let Φ(t) = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y denote the deterministic\n1303\tcomponent of the bridge state at time t (Eq. 4 with σt ϵ removed). Under Assumption 1, the K-step\n1304\tRiemann approximation ΦK of Φ along the non-uniform schedule tk = T (k/K)γ , γ ∈ (0, 1],\n1305\tsatisfies\n1306\tsup Φ(tk−1 ) − ΦK (tk−1 ) ≤ C K − min(1,γ) .\n1307\tk\n1308\t\n1309\tProof. Standard mean-value estimates on C 1 schedules yield local error |Φ(tk−1 ) − ΦK (tk−1 )| ≤\n1310\tC (∆tk ) ∥Φ′ ∥∞ on [tk−1 , tk ], where ∆tk = tk − tk−1 . For the schedule tk = T (k/K)γ ,\n1311\t\u0001\n1312\t∆tk = T (k/K)γ − ((k − 1)/K)γ ≤ T γK −γ k γ−1 .\n1313\tFor γ ≤ 1, k γ−1 is non-increasing in k, so supk ∆tk = ∆t1 ≤ T K −γ , giving the stated bound with\n1314\texponent min(1, γ) = γ.\n1315\tRemark. Front-loading (γ<1) does not improve the worst-case rate exponent. However, since\n1316\t∆tk ≤ T γK −γ k γ−1 is non-increasing in k for γ ≤ 1, the schedule concentrates many small\n1317\tsteps near tK = T , where the reverse rollout of Eq. 5 is initialized at xT = y and where per-step\n1318\terrors propagate through all subsequent reverse-sampling iterations. The single large step ∆t1 , by\n1319\tcontrast, traverses the low-t regime in which xt already lies close to x and x̂θ is most accurate.\n1320\tThis redistribution—not the asymptotic rate—is what empirically dominates small-K reconstruction\n1321\tquality.\n1322\t18\n1323\t\n1324\t\fC.4\n1325\t\n1326\tCombining the Two Sources of Error\n1327\t\n1328\tLet x̂(K) denote the random variable whose law is p̂K , and let X ∼ pbr\n1329\t0 . We construct a synchronous\n1330\tcoupling between x̂(K) and X by sharing the noise variables z across the K rollout steps with the\n1331\tcorresponding bridge perturbation ϵ in the continuous process. Under this coupling,\n1332\tK\n1333\tK\n1334\th\n1335\ti\n1336\tX\n1337\tY\n1338\tX\n1339\t\u00012\n1340\t(K)\n1341\t2\n1342\t2\n1343\tW22 (p̂K , pbr\n1344\t)\n1345\t≤\n1346\tE\n1347\t∥x̂\n1348\t−\n1349\tX∥\n1350\t≤\n1351\t2\n1352\t∆t\n1353\tE[δ\n1354\t]\n1355\t·\n1356\t(1\n1357\t+\n1358\tL\n1359\t∆t\n1360\t)\n1361\t+\n1362\t2\n1363\t∆tk · disck ,\n1364\tk\n1365\tk\n1366\tx\n1367\tj\n1368\t0\n1369\tk=1\n1370\t\n1371\tj<k\n1372\t\n1373\tk=1\n1374\t\n1375\twhere disck is the Riemann error from Lemma 2; the ∆tk weighting on the model-fidelity term tracks\n1376\tthe time-averaged definition of L⋆traj , L⋆path (Eqs. 8,7), so each step contributes proportionally to its\n1377\tQ\n1378\ttime interval. The Lipschitz factor j (1 + Lx ∆tj ) is uniformly bounded by exp(Lx T ), absorbing it\n1379\tP\n1380\tinto the constant. By Lemma 1 and k ∆tk = T , the first sum is bounded by C T (L⋆path + L⋆traj ),\n1381\tP\n1382\tindependent of K. By Lemma 2 together with k (∆tk )2 ≤ T supk ∆tk = T 2 K −γ ,√the second\n1383\t√\n1384\t√\n1385\tsum is bounded by C K −2 min(1,γ) . Taking square roots and applying a + b ≤ a + b yields\n1386\tq\n1387\t−α\n1388\tW2 (p̂K , pbr\n1389\t+ C2 L⋆path + L⋆traj ,\n1390\t0 ) ≤ C1 K\n1391\twhich is exactly Eq. (9).\n1392\tC.5\n1393\t\n1394\tJustification of Assumption 1\n1395\t\n1396\tWe address each of the three regularity conditions in turn and explain why each is reasonable in our\n1397\tsetting.\n1398\t(i) Lipschitz continuity of x̂θ . The condition that x̂θ (·, y, t) is Lx -Lipschitz in its first argument and\n1399\tLt -Lipschitz in t is standard in diffusion-sampling analyses [37, 26]. It is justified architecturally\n1400\tand empirically: (a) our denoiser is a 1D U-Net with GroupNorm [47] and SiLU activations, both\n1401\tLipschitz; (b) the convolutional layers’ Lipschitz constants are bounded since weights remain finite\n1402\tunder our AdamW training with weight decay 0.01 and gradient clipping at norm 1.0 (Appendix B);\n1403\t(c) timestep dependence enters only through a smooth sinusoidal embedding followed by FiLM\n1404\tconditioning, which is C ∞ in t and so trivially Lipschitz on the compact interval [0, T ]. We do\n1405\tnot enforce a target Lx explicitly (e.g., via spectral normalization); the assumption asserts that the\n1406\ttrained network is Lipschitz with some finite constant, which is automatic for any network without\n1407\tunbounded activations or singular layers. Tighter constant estimates—needed only for quantitatively\n1408\ttight versions of the bound—would require spectral norm tracking and are left to future work.\n1409\t(ii) Smoothness of the schedules. The cosine schedule β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 ((t/T +\n1410\ts)/(1 + s) · π/2) is in fact C ∞ on [0, T ], considerably stronger than the C 1 requirement: it is the\n1411\tcomposition of a smooth trigonometric function with an affine reparameterization. Its monotonicity\n1412\tfollows from cos2 being strictly decreasing on the relevant subinterval, and its first and second\n1413\tderivatives are uniformly bounded on [0, T ] since the schedule arguments stay in a compact\n1414\tp subset of\n1415\t[0, π/2] where cos and sin are bounded. The bridge-perturbation magnitude σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t )\n1416\tis C ∞ on the open interval (0, T ) and continuous on [0, T ] with σ0 = σT = 0; the square-root\n1417\tintroduces a one-sided Hölder singularity exactly at the endpoints, but standard SDE-discretization\n1418\targuments (e.g., [37]) accommodate this via a small endpoint truncation [ϵ0 , T − ϵ0 ] that vanishes in\n1419\tthe bound’s leading order. In our setting all sampling steps lie strictly in the interior of [0, T ] with\n1420\ttK = T at the noisy endpoint where the bridge is deterministic, so endpoint singularities do not affect\n1421\tthe discretization.\n1422\t(iii) Bounded second moments.\n1423\tE∥xt ∥2 < ∞ uniformly in t follows directly from\n1424\tp The condition\n1425\tp\n1426\tthe bridge construction xt = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ: by Jensen’s inequality and the triangle\n1427\tinequality,\n1428\t2\n1429\tE∥xt ∥2 ≤ 3E∥x∥2 + 3E∥y∥2 + 3σmax\n1430\td,\n1431\twhere d is the signal dimension and the right-hand side is finite because audio waveforms are bounded\n1432\t(∥x∥∞ , ∥y∥∞ ≤ 1 after our standard amplitude normalization, so E∥x∥2 ≤ d and similarly for y).\n1433\tFor E∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥2 , the Lipschitz condition (i) gives ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥ ≤ Lx ∥xt ∥ + |x̂θ (0, y, t)|, so\n1434\tfiniteness of the second moment of x̂θ follows from finiteness of E∥xt ∥2 together with boundedness\n1435\tof x̂θ at the reference input xt = 0 (a finite-norm constant for any fixed trained network on a fixed\n1436\t19\n1437\t\n1438\t\fPESQ\n1439\t\n1440\tSTOI (%)\n1441\t\n1442\tDomestic\n1443\t\n1444\tNatural\n1445\t\n1446\tOffice\n1447\t\n1448\tPublic\n1449\t\n1450\tTransportation\n1451\t\n1452\t96.6\n1453\t3.92\n1454\t\n1455\tPESQ\n1456\t\n1457\t95.8\n1458\t\n1459\t3.84\n1460\t\n1461\t3.80\n1462\t\n1463\tSTOI (%)\n1464\t\n1465\t96.2\n1466\t\n1467\t3.88\n1468\t\n1469\tPublic\n1470\t\n1471\tUS\n1472\tTB\n1473\t\n1474\tTC\n1475\tAR\n1476\t\n1477\tO\n1478\tET\n1479\tR\n1480\tTM\n1481\t\n1482\tER\n1483\tET\n1484\tPC\n1485\tAF\n1486\t\n1487\tN\n1488\t\n1489\tES\n1490\tTO\n1491\tPR\n1492\t\n1493\tG\n1494\t\n1495\tOffice\n1496\t\n1497\tPS\n1498\tTAT\n1499\tIO\n1500\t\n1501\tTIN\n1502\tEE\n1503\t\n1504\tICE\n1505\t\n1506\tNatural\n1507\t\n1508\tOM\n1509\t\n1510\tFF\n1511\tOO\n1512\t\n1513\tNF\n1514\t\n1515\tIEL\n1516\t\n1517\tD\n1518\t\n1519\tK\n1520\tNP\n1521\tAR\n1522\t\n1523\tIVE\n1524\tR\n1525\t\n1526\tN\n1527\t\n1528\tDomestic\n1529\t\n1530\tNR\n1531\t\n1532\tITC\n1533\tHE\n1534\tDK\n1535\t\n1536\tNG\n1537\tIVI\n1538\tDL\n1539\t\n1540\tDW\n1541\tAS\n1542\tHIN\n1543\t\n1544\tG\n1545\t\n1546\t95.4\n1547\t\n1548\tTransportation\n1549\t\n1550\tFigure 5: Scene-stratified performance across all 14 noise types in VoiceBank+DEMAND. Inner\n1551\tring: PESQ; outer ring: STOI (%). Colors indicate scene categories: Domestic (blue), Nature (green),\n1552\tOffice (orange), Public (red), Transport (purple). For reference, the strongest baseline ROSE-CD\n1553\t[33] achieves a mean PESQ of 3.85 across these conditions; our method exceeds this in every scene,\n1554\twith PESQ ranging from 3.84 to 3.92 (mean 3.88, standard deviation <0.03 across scenes). Y-axis\n1555\tranges are zoomed for visibility of cross-scene variation.\n1556\tobservation y). Both moments are therefore bounded by a constant depending only on Lx , σmax , d,\n1557\tand the amplitude bound on the data.\n1558\tIn summary, Assumption 1 encodes mild structural properties that hold automatically for our cosinescheduled SB construction with a standard U-Net denoiser trained on amplitude-normalized waveforms. The assumption fails only in pathological regimes (unbounded data, divergent network outputs,\n1559\tor non-smooth schedules) that are not relevant to our setting or to standard SE practice.\n1560\tC.6\n1561\t\n1562\tDiscussion\n1563\t\n1564\tA few caveats deserve mention. First, Assumption 1(i) is a Lipschitz condition on the trained network;\n1565\tit can be enforced via spectral normalization in practice, but in our experiments it is assumed rather\n1566\tthan explicitly imposed. Second, the bound is presented as a design-justifying inequality; it is loose\n1567\tin the sense that the constants C1 , C2 involve worst-case Lipschitz factors and are not numerically\n1568\testimated here. A tighter quantitative match to empirical PESQ-vs-K would require finer Lipschitz\n1569\testimates on the U-Net and is left for future work. Third, the bound shows that small K is justified\n1570\tconditional on the regularizers reaching small training-time values; if a model is trained without these\n1571\tterms, the second term of Eq. (9) does not vanish and the bound cannot guarantee small-K quality.\n1572\tThis is consistent with the role we ascribe to Lpath + Ltraj in the main paper.\n1573\t\n1574\tD\n1575\t\n1576\tScene-Stratified Performance\n1577\t\n1578\tFigure 5 breaks down enhancement performance across all 14 noise environments. Natural environments (NPARK, NRIVER, NFIELD) achieve the highest scores due to relatively stable spectral\n1579\tcharacteristics, while public spaces (PCAFETER, PSTATION, PRESTO) are slightly more challenging due to non-stationary crowd noise and unpredictable transient events. Despite this variation, the\n1580\tspread is small (PESQ standard deviation <0.03), indicating that the heterogeneous expert routing\n1581\tautomatically adapts to different acoustic contexts without per-scene tuning or explicit scene labels.\n1582\tThis consistent cross-scene performance complements the SNR-stratified analysis in Figure 3d of the\n1583\tmain paper, together demonstrating robust enhancement across both noise type and noise level.\n1584\t\n1585\tE\n1586\t\n1587\tQualitative Visualization\n1588\t\n1589\tFigure 6 shows representative spectrograms before and after enhancement. The denoised output\n1590\texhibits clearer harmonic structures, sharper formant contours, and significantly attenuated broadband\n1591\tnoise, consistent with the objective gains reported in Table 1. Note that some high-frequency\n1592\t20\n1593\t\n1594\t\fFigure 6: Spectrogram comparison before and after enhancement. HybridSB-MoE attenuates\n1595\tbroadband noise while preserving speech harmonics and formant structure. Color bar (dB) is shown\n1596\ton the right.\n1597\tcomponents in the enhanced spectrogram appear more pronounced than in the noisy input; this is\n1598\tbecause heavy broadband noise in the input masks underlying speech harmonics that become visible\n1599\tonly after suppression—it reflects accurate harmonic recovery, not bandwidth extension or content\n1600\tfabrication.\n1601\t\n1602\t21\n1603\t\n1604\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "0e9433d4-c325-4618-bb42-c85dd5d271b1", "uuid": "dbd4ef47-fc8e-4256-b6fc-9c6dc9775030", "timestamp": "2026-08-14T07:24:04.052Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.12715.txt", "content": "HybridSB-MoE: Dual-Domain Schrödinger Bridges\nwith Scene-Adaptive Expert Routing for Speech\nEnhancement\n\narXiv:2608.12715v1 [cs.SD] 13 Aug 2026\n\nZhengyi Lu Aswini Sivakumar Jie Hu Yao Qiang\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering, Oakland University\nRochester, MI 48309\n{zhengyilu, aswinisivakumar, jiehu, qiang}@oakland.edu\n\nAbstract\nGenerative speech enhancement faces three gaps: spectral models capture harmonic\nstructure but often disrupt phase, waveform models preserve phase but miss harmonics, and Schrödinger Bridges (SB) shorten transport from noise to clean speech\nbut leave inference cost only loosely tied to training. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\na dual-domain framework that fills these gaps through three contributions unified\nby a single asymmetric design principle. (i) Asymmetric uncertainty fusion: The\nspectral path captures epistemic uncertainty via expert disagreement, while the\nwaveform bridge models aleatoric variance through stochastic dynamics. We fuse\nthem asymmetrically, allowing the mixing weight to adapt to distinct error regimes\nrather than average predictions. (ii) Heterogeneous MoE with top-k=2 routing\nacross five distinct architectural archetypes, where architectural diversity makes the\nepistemic signal indicate which inductive bias fails rather than small perturbations\namong similar experts. (iii) Discretization bound (Theorem 1): path-consistency\nand trajectory regularizers together bound the K-step bridge sampling error in\n2-Wasserstein distance at rate K −α , making small-K inference an objective-level\nguarantee rather than an empirical claim. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, HybridSBMoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at their step budgets while\nremaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nSpeech enhancement (SE) has long faced a structural domain trade-off. Spectral methods are\neffective at modeling harmonic structure and stationary noise, but often fragment phase across STFT\nframes; waveform methods preserve phase continuity, but can struggle with harmonically structured\ninterference (Fig. 1). This trade-off becomes especially limiting in real-world deployment, where\na hearing aid on a moving bus, a video-conferencing system in a crowded café, or an in-car voice\nassistant must handle harmonic engine drone, broadband ambient hum, and non-stationary crowd\nbabble under millisecond latency budgets and without scene labels at inference time [1, 2]. These\nsettings require more than a universally stronger model: they require a mechanism that can exploit\ncomplementary inductive biases and decide which one to trust for each input.\nA decade of deep learning has advanced SE from DNN masking [3, 4], through time-domain\n[5, 2, 6] and spectro-temporal [7, 8] architectures to diffusion-based generative models [9, 10, 11] and\nSchrödinger Bridge formulations that replace the uninformative Gaussian prior of diffusion with direct\ntransport from noisy to clean speech. Despite this progress, three structural limitations persist that are\nprecisely what a dual-domain framework must address. (i) Single-domain commitment. Existing\nmethods typically operate in either the waveform or spectral domain, sacrificing the complementary\ninductive bias of the other [8]. (ii) Uniform processing across heterogeneous noise. A single\nPreprint.\n\n\fnetwork handles stationary appliance hum, harmonic engine noise, and non-stationary crowd babble\nalike, despite their structurally different inductive biases [12, 13]. (iii) Loosely controlled sampling\ncost. Generative SE pipelines often require many iterative refinement steps, with limited formal\nconnection between the training objective and the inference budget [10, 11].\nThe natural response, i.e., running a spectral and a waveform pathway in parallel and combining\nthem, fails on its own, because a generic ensemble of two prediction streams provides no mechanism\nto know which pathway is failing on a given input. Without such signal, the combination defaults\nto fixed-weight averaging, which is provably suboptimal whenever the two branches’ errors are\nuncorrelated, and the dual-domain promise reduces to a parameter-count increase.\nWe introduce HybridSB-MoE, a dual-domain framework organized around one asymmetric idea: a multiexpert spectral path naturally produces an epistemic uncertainty signal (which expert is right?), and a stochastic\nwaveform bridge naturally produces an aleatoric one\n(intrinsic transport noise). These are categorically different signals about categorically different errors. Pairing\nthe two pathways and fusing on this asymmetry yields\na mixing weight that selects between two error regimes\nrather than averaging two predictions, transforming the\ndual-domain promise into a principled co-design. Two\nfurther architectural choices are required for this idea to\nwork. First, the spectral pathway must produce informative expert disagreement, which requires architectural Figure 1: The persistent dual-domain diheterogeneity rather than capacity-replicated variation. chotomy in speech enhancement. WaveWe therefore use heterogeneous expert archetypes, each form processing preserves temporal fine\naligned with a canonical noise-processing primitive, in- structure and phase coherence but undercluding low-rank denoising, wide receptive fields, in- performs on harmonically structured information bottlenecks, harmonic bases, and universal terference; spectral processing captures\napproximation, and combine them under sparse routing. harmonic structure and stationary noise\nSecond, the waveform pathway must remain faithful un- patterns but fragments phase across STFT\nder the small inference budget K targeted by the system. frames. The two domains succeed and fail\nWe therefore train it with path-consistency and trajectory in opposite regimes, and existing generaregularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that jointly min- tive methods inherit one half of the tradeimizing them bounds the K-step bridge discretization off by committing to a single domain.\nerror in 2-Wasserstein distance, making small-K inference a consequence of the training objective\nrather than an empirical heuristic. In Section 3, we further show that no proper subset of these three\ncomponents recovers the central design property.\nOur contributions are as follows:\n1. A dual-domain framework built around asymmetric uncertainty. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\na dual-domain framework that couples spectral MoE and waveform SB pathways. By pairing each\npath with its natural uncertainty signal, epistemic disagreement for spectral experts and aleatoric\nvariance for the waveform bridge, HybridSB-MoE enables fusion that adapts to error regimes rather\nthan fixed-weight averaging.\n2. A heterogeneous spectral MoE for informative epistemic disagreement. We design a sparse\ntop-k=2 spectral MoE with architecturally distinct expert archetypes. This heterogeneity makes\nexpert disagreement reflect mismatched inductive biases rather than small perturbations among\ncapacity-replicated experts.\n3. A regularized waveform bridge for few-step inference. We train the waveform SB with\npath consistency and trajectory regularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that they bound the K-step\ndiscretization error in 2-Wasserstein distance, linking small-K inference to the training objective.\n4. SOTA on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE improves over diffusion- and SB-based\nbaselines at their respective step budgets, remains competitive with consistency-distilled few-step\nmethods, and provides calibrated fusion uncertainty for input-adaptive dual-domain enhancement.\n\n2\n\n\f2\n\nRelated Work\n\nDiscriminative SE. The field has progressed from DNN masking [3, 4] that surpassed classical\nWiener and MMSE estimators [14, 15], through time-domain encoder–decoders (Conv-TasNet [5, 16],\nDEMUCS [2]), dual-path sequence models [6], full-sub-band hybrids (FullSubNet+ [7]), spectrotemporal grids (TF-GridNet [8]), and recent Mamba-based linear-complexity backbones [17, 18].\nJoint enhancement objectives that combine SI-SDR with perceptual surrogates push fidelity further\n[19, 20], yet the underlying paradigm remains a one-shot deterministic regression. Two limitations\ntherefore persist regardless of architecture. (i) Inherent ambiguity: under severe non-stationary\nor harmonically structured noise, the noisy-to-clean mapping admits multiple plausible solutions,\nand a deterministic regressor must collapse this multimodal posterior to a single point estimate,\nwhich typically manifests as residual noise or over-suppressed harmonics [21]. (ii) No uncertainty:\ndiscriminative networks expose no signal at inference time to flag this ambiguity, so a downstream\nmodule cannot tell when the prediction should be trusted—making them unsuitable as one branch of\na confidence-routed dual-domain combiner.\nGenerative models and Schrödinger Bridges for SE. Likelihood-based [21] and score-based\n[9, 22, 23] diffusion models reverse a noise corruption process; SGMSE/SGMSE+ [10, 11] apply\nthis in the complex spectrogram domain, jointly modeling magnitude and phase, but require many\niterative steps from an uninformative Gaussian prior. Two recent paradigms exploit the structure of\nthe noisy observation directly. Flow-matching approaches [24, 25] regress velocity fields between\ncoupled distributions, shortening the sampling trajectory but typically without an explicit stochastic\nperturbation. Schrödinger Bridges [26, 27] learn the optimal-transport-like coupling between the\nnoisy observation and clean speech, retaining a small bridge variance for full-support marginals.\nSB for SE [28, 29, 30, 31, 32] confirm improved low-SNR structure preservation along this shorter\npath. Empirical step-count reductions have been achieved by consistency distillation [33, 34],\nadversarial few-step training [35], and SB-consistency trajectory models [36]. None of these works\nlinks the training objective to the inference budget, where small K is asserted but not derived.\nTheoretical convergence analyses for diffusion sampling exist in the broader literature [37], but their\nbounds are stated in terms of the score-matching error rather than tractable, training-time-minimized\nregularizers, and have not been adapted to the doubly-conditioned SB setting. Moreover, these\nmethods remain single-domain and lack calibrated uncertainty for fusion. We address both with\nasymmetric uncertainty fusion and a discretization bound (Theorem 1) that links small-K inference\nto explicit training-time quantities.\nMixture-of-Experts for SE. MoE enables conditional computation with sparse routing, scaling\ncapacity without proportional inference cost [38, 39, 40]. In SE, sparse MoE [12] first enabled\nscene-adaptive processing via noise-clustered experts; zero-shot personalization [41] extended this to\nspeakers via embedding-based routing; clean-cluster pre-training [13] enforced expert specialization\nthrough pre-training initialization; and dynamic capacity allocation [42] improved routing efficiency\nat inference. However, this line of work shares three limitations. (i) Homogeneity. Existing methods\nreplicate a single architecture across experts, assuming one computational primitive can handle tonal,\nambient, harmonic, and crowd noise. This assumption breaks down under realistic, structurally\ndiverse noise. (ii) Routing without uncertainty. Existing routing only partitions inputs into specialist\nregions, rather than exposing interpretable uncertainty; thus, the epistemic signal from multi-expert\ndisagreement remains unused. (iii) Separation from generative SE. MoE routing and generative bridge\nmodels remain disconnected in SE, leaving scene-adaptive computation and structured distributional\ntransport as separate lines of work. We address all three with heterogeneous archetype experts that\nencode distinct noise-specific inductive biases. Their disagreement provides the epistemic signal\nwhich, together with the SB’s aleatoric variance, drives asymmetric fusion. To our knowledge, this is\nthe first joint MoE–SB framework for SE.\nUncertainty quantification and dual-branch fusion. Calibrated uncertainty for deep regression is\nmost commonly obtained via deep ensembles [43] or learned heteroscedastic variance heads, with\nclassification calibration assessed via ECE [44]. Prior dual-branch SE methods that combine timeand frequency-domain estimates typically use either fixed-weight averaging or learned attention over\npredictions [8, 2]; none assigns categorically distinct uncertainty types to the two branches, and so the\nfusion cannot route on which kind of error each branch is exposed to. Our pathway-typed asymmetric\nfusion is, to our knowledge, the first to exploit this structure, and the calibration loss (Eq. (11)) ties\nthe abstract epistemic/aleatoric labels to measurable per-pathway reconstruction errors so the routing\nweight is learned, not asserted.\n3\n\n\fFigure 2: Overview of HybridSB-MoE. The spectral pathway (top) routes log-magnitude features\nz = log |S{y}| through N heterogeneous archetype experts via gating G(z) to produce xspec (t).\nThe waveform pathway (bottom) iteratively refines an SB state [xt , y] through a U-Net to produce\nxwave (t). An uncertainty-aware fusion combines both via uepi (top-k expert disagreement) and uale\n(bridge-variance head) to yield x̂(t).\n\n3\n\nMethod\n\nHybridSB-MoE has three coupled components: a heterogeneous spectral MoE, an SB waveform\npathway with path-consistency and trajectory regularizers (for which we prove a K-step discretization\nbound, Theorem 1), and an asymmetric uncertainty fusion that selects between epistemic and aleatoric\nerror regimes. Figure 2 illustrates the overall HybridSB-MoE framework. We develop each component\nin turn and close the section by showing that no proper subset recovers the central design property.\nOverview. Given a noisy input y(t), HybridSB-MoE processes it through two parallel pathways\ndesigned to capture complementary speech characteristics. The spectral pathway applies the heterogeneous MoE to the log-magnitude STFT for scene-adaptive enhancement. In parallel, the waveform\npathway runs an efficient SB on the time-domain signal to preserve phase. The two pathways exhibit\nasymmetric reliability across acoustic conditions: spectral processing excels in harmonically structured noise, whereas waveform processing is more robust in phase-sensitive scenarios. Consequently,\na fixed fusion strategy is inherently suboptimal. We therefore introduce an uncertainty-aware fusion\nmodule that integrates xspec (t) and xwave (t) by dynamically weighting each pathway based on\nconfidence, producing the final enhanced signal x̂(t). Both pathways run in parallel at inference, and\nlong utterances use overlap-add segmentation (Appendix B).\nSpectral feature extraction. We apply the STFT [45] to obtain the complex spectrogram S{y} ∈\nCF ×Tf (F frequency bins) and decompose it in polar form into magnitude |S{y}| and phase ∠S{y},\nprocessed by separate heads (below). The MoE consumes log-magnitude features z = log |S{y}| ∈\nRF ×Tf , whose logarithmic scaling compresses the dynamic range in line with human hearing.\nHeterogeneous mixture-of-experts. Unlike conventional homogeneous MoE designs [38, 39, 40] in\nwhich all experts share the same architecture and differ only in learned weights, we instantiate distinct\narchitectural archetypes, each tailored to a canonical noise-processing primitive, i.e., Home, Nature,\nOffice, Transport, and Public for VoiceBank+DEMAND (see Appendix A for full specification).\nUnder sparse top-k routing, pairwise mixtures and weighted blends of these archetypes cover the 14\nnoise categories in VoiceBank+DEMAND [46] combinatorially rather than by a dedicated expert\nper noise, and the design extends to new acoustic environments by adding archetypes per noise\nfamily encountered, without altering the rest of the framework (see Appendix A for details). All\nexperts share a backbone of normalization (group norm [47]) → variable internal modules →\nbottleneck projection, ensuring routing-interface compatibility while permitting structurally divergent\nintermediate computation. This design, a small semantically anchored basis composed of sparse\n4\n\n\frouting, replaces the parameter scaling of large homogeneous MoEs with interpretable routing and\nmeaningful expert disagreement, which we use for asymmetric fusion below.\nScene-adaptive sparse routing. Standard sparse routing [38, 39] assigns each input token to its\ntop-k experts independently, which is well-suited to language modelling but ill-suited to SE: the\ndominant noise type is a global property of an utterance, while local acoustic events (a phoneme onset,\na transient hit) require frame-level adaptivity. We therefore use a two-level gate: Archetype-level\nrouting Garch (z) pools z across time and decides the dominant archetype mixture for the whole\nutterance; token-level routing Gtoken (z) refines this assignment per frame. The two combine as\nG(z) = αGarch (z) + (1 − α)Gtoken (z) with α ∈ [0, 1]. An MLP maps G(z) to per-expert logits;\nthe top-k entries are softmax-renormalized to weights {Gi (z)}i∈Ik , and the routed output is\nX\nx̂spec =\nGi (z) Ei (z),\n(1)\ni∈Ik\n\nwhere Ik indexes the selected experts and Ei are expert networks. To prevent expert collapse [38, 39],\na standard load-balancing regularizer\nLaux = λI Var(pi ) + λL Var(ni )\n\n(2)\n\npenalizes uneven utilization, where pi and ni denote the average routing probability and token count\nof expert i, respectively.\nSpectral reconstruction. The routed features x̂spec pass through two parallel 1 × 1-conv heads.\nSince phase cannot be inferred from log-magnitude alone, the phase head also consumes the noisy\nphase as (sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y}), concatenated along the channel dimension:\nM̂ = Mmax sigmoid(hmag (x̂spec )), ∆ϕ = ϕmax tanh(hpha (x̂spec , sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y})), (3)\nwhere Mmax , ϕmax bound the mask and phase correction to prevent over-suppression, excessive\namplification, and phase-wrap instability. The enhanced spectrum Ŝ = M̂ ⊙ |S{y}| · ej(∠S{y}+∆ϕ)\nis inverted to xspec (t) via iSTFT with overlap-add. STFT processing nevertheless fragments temporal\nfine structure through frame-wise phase decoupling, motivating the parallel waveform pathway below.\nSchrödinger Bridge formulation. The continuous-time SB problem seeks the stochastic process\nminimizing the KL divergence to a reference Brownian motion subject to fixed marginal distributions\nat both endpoints [26, 27, 48]. In our application the two endpoints are the noisy observation y\nand the clean target x, so the bridge directly couples the conditional distributions our system must\ntransport between. Where standard diffusion [9] traverses the full path from a data-independent\nGaussian prior, the bridge exploits the substantial mutual information between y and x to yield a\nshorter, more informative trajectory and, empirically, fewer sampling steps and better low-SNR\nstructure preservation [28, 29, 30]. Conceptually adjacent flow-matching methods [24, 25] also\ncouple paired distributions but typically without an explicit bridge perturbation; our SB construction\nretains a small noise term σt ϵ to keep the marginal p(xt | x, y) full-support, which is required for\nscore-based reverse sampling and for the trajectory regularizer to act as a self-consistency constraint.\nThe intermediate state at diffusion time t ∈ [0, T ] interpolates between y and x with a small Gaussian\nperturbation,\nq\nq\nxt =\n\nβ̄t x +\n\n1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ,\n\nϵ ∼ N (0, I),\n\n(4)\n\nwhere β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 ((t/T +s)/(1+s)·π/2) is a cumulative cosine p\nschedule [49]\ndecreasing monotonically from β̄0 = 1 to β̄T = 0 (offset s=0.008), and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t )\n(σmax =0.05) vanishes at both endpoints, so x0 = x and xT = y deterministically while σt ϵ supplies\nthe full-support marginal p(xt | x, y) required by the score-based reverse process.\nThe reverse process is parameterized as a learnable denoiser x̂θ (xt , y, t) that predicts the clean target\ndirectly from the bridge state and the observation. Rather than solving the reverse-time stochastic\ndifferential equation (SDE) associated with the bridge in closed form, we adopt a tractable bridgeconsistent update that iteratively re-injects the predicted clean estimate into the forward construction\nof Eq. (4) at the previous timestep:\nq\nq\nxt−1 = β̄t−1 x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t−1 y + σt−1 z,\nz ∼ N (0, I).\n(5)\n5\n\n\fThis data-prediction update is the bridge analogue of DDPM/DDIM ancestral sampling [9, 23]: deterministic when σt−1 =0, stochastic otherwise. Bridge-marginal consistency is enforced empirically\nthrough the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced below. The denoiser is a 1D\nU-Net with transformer bottleneck and FiLM timestep conditioning (Appendix B). The training\nobjective is the data-prediction loss:\n2\n\nLdata\nSB = Et,(x,y),ϵ x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x 2 ,\n\n(6)\n\naugmented by the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced next.\nPath-consistency and trajectory regularizers. A fixed front-loaded schedule tk = T (k/K)γ with\nγ<1 concentrates the K-step budget in the early-trajectory regime where the SNR changes fastest and\nwhere errors propagate to all later steps [9]. Two regularizers then encourage a smooth, low-curvature\ndenoising path in the (y, x) subspace. The path-consistency loss enforces cross-timestep agreement\nof clean-signal predictions along the same trajectory:\nh\ni\n2\nLpath = Et,t′ ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ )∥\n(7)\nwhere t, t′ are independently sampled along the same bridge trajectory. This penalizes inconsistent\nclean-signal predictions across timesteps, reducing reverse-process curvature (cf. consistency models\n[34], but here for path smoothing, not distillation). The trajectory loss anchors xt to the scheduleconsistent reconstruction:\n\"\n#\nq\nq\n\u0001 2\nLtraj = Et xt −\nβ̄t x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t y\n.\n(8)\nAt the optimum x̂θ = x, the residual reduces to σt ϵ, so Ltraj couples the prediction to the specific\nbridge state xt at each timestep, complementing the in-expectation regression of Eq. (6). At inference,\nwe use K=8 steps with the front-loaded non-uniform discretization tk = T (k/K)γ , γ=0.6 (see\nAppendix B for more details).\nDiscretization bound. The two regularizers above make small-K inference viable, and we now show\nwhy. Let p̂K be the law of the K-step rollout of Eq. (5) at t=0 and pbr\n0 the continuous-time bridge\nmarginal at t=0, both conditional on y.\nAssumption 1 (Regularity).\np (i) x̂θ (·, y, t) is Lx -Lipschitz in its first argument and Lt -Lipschitz in\nt. (ii) β̄t and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t ) are C 1 on [0, T ], with β̄t monotone and bounded second\nderivative. (iii) E∥xt ∥2 and E∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥2 are uniformly bounded in t.\nTheorem 1 (K-step bridge discretization bound). Under Assumption 1, with the schedule tk =\nT (k/K)γ , γ ∈ (0, 1],\nq\n\u0001\n−α\nW2 p̂K , pbr\n≤\nC\nK\n+\nC\nL⋆path + L⋆traj ,\n(9)\n1\n2\n0\nwith α = min(1, γ), L⋆ the training-termination values of Eq. (7)–(8), and C1 , C2 depending only\non Lx , Lt , σmax , and the curvature of β̄t .\nWith α = min(1, γ), front-loading (γ < 1) concentrates steps near t=0 where Φ′ is largest, which\nis what drives small-K behavior empirically.\nEach term corresponds to a separate lever: C1 K −α is\n√\ncontrolled by inference-time K, γ; C2 L⋆ is controlled by training-time minimization of the two\n⋆\nregularizers and is independent of K.\n√ Two consequences follow. First, once L is small, the bound\n−α\nsaturates: any K with C1 K ≲ C2 L⋆ already attains the asymptotic floor, which is what justifies\nour K=8 rather than the much larger K of standard diffusion sampling. Second, the regularizers\nare not auxiliary terms; without them, the second term has no training-time upper bound, and the\ninequality does not exist as a function of K. Therefore, removing either breaks the entire argument.\nThis is the formal sense in which path-consistency and trajectory regularization are load-bearing\nfor our inference schedule. Eq. (9) is closely related to recent convergence results for diffusion\nsampling [37] but adapts those analyses to the doubly-conditioned bridge process and, importantly,\nreplaces score-matching error with the explicit, training-time-minimized regularizer pair, which\nmakes the bound directly actionable as a design tool. The full proof includes a one-step fidelity\nlemma combined with a non-uniform Riemann estimate under a synchronous coupling, with details\ndeferred to Appendix C. We present Eq. (9) as a design-justifying inequality, not a tight predictor.\n6\n\n\fAsymmetric uncertainty fusion. A naive dual-branch ensembler treats the two pathways symmetrically; ours does not, by design. Epistemic uncertainty (‘which expert is right?’) is well-defined only\nwhen multiple experts coexist (the spectral MoE); the waveform pathway has no analog. Aleatoric\nuncertainty (intrinsic stochasticity of the bridge SDE in Eq. (4)) is well-defined only when the\nforward process is genuinely stochastic (the waveform pathway); the deterministic spectral mask in\nEq. (3) has no analog. Each pathway therefore carries exactly one uncertainty type: uepi flags an\narchitectural mismatch, uale flags a stochastic transport error. The fusion selects\nP between these two\nerror regimes; it does not average two predictions. Concretely, uepi = kT1 f i∈Ik ∥Ei (z)− Ē(z)∥22\nP\nwith Ē(z) = k1 i∈Ik Ei (z) (deep-ensembles sense [43], not Bayesian posterior); uale is from the\nU-Net’s per-sample log-variance head, exponentiated and time-averaged. After z-score normalization,\na 2-layer MLP gives w = σ(MLP(ũepi , ũale )) ∈ [0, 1], and\nx̂(t) = w · xspec (t) + (1 − w) · xwave (t).\n\n(10)\n\nHigh expert disagreement pushes w toward the waveform pathway and high SB variance toward the\nspectral pathway: each pathway is deferred away from when its native error is large. A calibration\nloss anchors the two scalars to the errors they should track,\nLcal = (uepi − ∥xspec − x∥22 )2 + (uale − ∥xwave − x∥22 )2 ,\n\n(11)\n\nwhere uepi , uale are pre-normalized; without it, the scalars have no incentive to scale with error, and\nthe fusion’s dependence on them would be arbitrary.\nTraining. All components are trained end-to-end. Each step runs both pathways in parallel: the\nspectral path produces xspec , uepi ; the waveform path samples t, ϵ, forms xt via Eq. (4), and outputs\nxwave , uale . The fusion yields x̂. The objective combines reconstruction, SB, load-balancing, and\ncalibration losses:\nL = Lrec + λSB LSB + λaux Laux + λcal Lcal ,\n(12)\n2\ndata\nwith Lrec = ∥x̂ − x∥2 and LSB = LSB + λpath Lpath + λtraj Ltraj . Loss weights (gridsearched on a 10% VB split): λSB =1.0, λpath =0.1, λtraj =0.05, λaux =0.01, λcal =0.05; ceilings\nMmax =5.0, ϕmax =π/4. Full procedure in Appendix B.\nWhy the components are coupled, not stacked. The central design property—a fusion that routes\non epistemic vs. aleatoric error regimes at a sampling budget derived from training—requires all\nthree components and survives no proper subset. Three counterfactuals make this concrete. (C1)\nSymmetric uncertainty heads on both pathways (the standard dual-branch ensemble): the fusion sees\ntwo aleatoric scalars and no epistemic one, making it blind to spectral expert disagreement. Thus,\nthe mixing weight collapses into a generic confidence average, consistent with the 0.17 PESQ drop\nobserved for “w/o uncertainty fusion” in Figure 3a. (C2) Homogeneous MoE in place of distinct\narchetypes: top-k blends span one function class instead of distinct inductive biases. In this way,\nuepi no longer indicates which bias is mismatched but only weight perturbations among identicalarchitecture experts, resulting in 0.43 PESQ drop as shown in Figure 3a. (C3) Drop the regularizers:\nTheorem 1’s second term loses its training-time upper bound, so small-K inference is no longer\nderivable; uale inherits the failure since the SB error it was calibrated against is now uncontrolled.\nRemoving the SB pathway entirely, leading to −0.63 PESQ, is the limit of this collapse. In all three\ncases the asymmetric fusion reduces to a fixed-weight or generic ensemble, the small-K guarantee\ndisappears, or both: the MoE produces the error only uepi detects, the regularized SB produces the\nerror only uale detects, Theorem 1 controls the SB-side error at the deployment budget, and the\nasymmetric fusion is the only mechanism that routes between them. The ablation pattern in Figure 3a\nof Section 4 is the empirical signature of this coupling.\n\n4\n\nExperiments and Discussion\n\nSetup. Dataset: We evaluate our method on VoiceBank+DEMAND [50, 46], a widely used benchmark containing 11,572 training and 824 test utterances from 28 and 2 speakers, respectively. Clean\nspeech is mixed with 14 noise types. All audio signals are resampled to 16 kHz. Metrics: We\nreport PESQ [51] and STOI [52] for perceptual quality and intelligibility, composite metrics CSIG,\nCBAK, and COVL [53] for signal/background/overall quality, Expected Calibration Error (ECE) [44]\nfor uncertainty calibration, and Real-Time Factor (RTF) with end-to-end latency for computational\nefficiency. Implementation: We use STFT with 1024-point FFT, 256-sample hop (16 ms), and Hann\nwindow, yielding 513 frequency bins. The MoE comprises 5 archetype experts (one per scene family\n7\n\n\fTable 1: Performance comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. Best results in bold (ties bolded jointly).\nAll baseline numbers are reproduced by us under a unified evaluation protocol. Our method achieves\nthe best score on every metric, with calibrated uncertainty estimates as an additional benefit.\nMethod\n\nType\n\nPESQ↑\n\nSTOI↑\n\nCSIG↑\n\nCBAK↑\n\nCOVL↑\n\nNoisy\n\n–\n\n1.97\n\n0.91\n\n–\n\n–\n\n–\n\nDiscriminative\nDiscriminative\nDiscriminative\n\n2.16\n3.55\n3.73\n\n0.92\n0.96\n0.96\n\n–\n4.79\n4.82\n\n–\n3.63\n3.67\n\n–\n4.37\n4.40\n\nSGMSE+ [11]\nSBCTM [36]\nSB-SE [28]\nROSE-CD [33]\n\nDiffusion\nSB-based\nSB-based\nDistillation\n\n3.45\n3.58\n3.70\n3.85\n\n0.95\n0.95\n0.95\n0.96\n\n4.71\n4.66\n4.77\n4.63\n\n3.64\n3.43\n3.75\n3.37\n\n4.31\n4.52\n4.48\n4.30\n\nHybridSB-MoE (Ours)\n\nHybrid\n\n3.88\n\n0.96\n\n4.82\n\n3.85\n\n4.82\n\nSEGAN [4]\nSEMamba [17]\nMamba-SEUNet [18]\n\nin VoiceBank+DEMAND, see Appendix A) with Top-k=2 routing. The waveform U-Net has 4\nencoder/decoder levels with a transformer bottleneck. Training uses AdamW (learning rate 2 × 10−4 ,\ncosine schedule, 200 epochs, batch size 32) on 2 NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs. Full hyperparameters\nare listed in Appendix B. Baselines: We compare against discriminative methods (SEGAN [4],\nSEMamba [17], Mamba-SEUNet [18]), diffusion/SB-based generative models (SGMSE+ [11], SBSE [28], SBCTM [36]), and the consistency-distilled ROSE-CD [33]. All baselines are retrained from\nscratch using their official implementations under a unified protocol (matched preprocessing, 16 kHz\nsampling, train/test split), and all reported metrics are computed by us with a shared evaluation script\nto avoid implementation-induced discrepancies.\nMain results. Table 1 reports the comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE attains\nthe best score on every metric, strictly dominating on PESQ, CBAK, and COVL and tying for the\nlead on STOI and CSIG. Two clarifications anchor the comparison. (i) We do not claim the smallest\nsampling budget as ROSE-CD reaches a single step via consistency distillation. At our K=8 we\nstrictly dominate SGMSE+, SB-SE, and SBCTM at their larger budgets, and exceed ROSE-CD across\nall five quality metrics despite its smaller K. (ii) The CBAK gain is the most informative single\nnumber, i.e., +0.10 over the strongest SB baseline (SB-SE) and +0.48 over ROSE-CD, indicating\nthat dual-domain processing suppresses background noise more effectively than either pure-spectral\nor pure-waveform generative pipelines. At the same time, the COVL gain (4.82 vs. 4.52) confirms\nthis without sacrificing signal fidelity. Furthermore, the choice K=8 is not heuristic. By Theorem 1,\nonce L⋆path + L⋆traj is minimized, the C1 K −α term saturates at modest K, see Appendix E.\nAblation studies. Figure 3a reports the ablation results. The performance drops follow the asymmetric\nuncertainty design. Removing the SB pathway causes the largest degradation (−0.63 PESQ), since the\nmodel loses both waveform-domain phase coherence and the aleatoric signal needed for asymmetric\nfusion. Removing the MoE module yields a smaller but still substantial drop (−0.43 PESQ), as the\nSB pathway remains active but the complementary epistemic signal from expert disagreement is lost.\nReplacing uncertainty-aware fusion with equal weighting also reduces performance (−0.17 PESQ),\nshowing that both pathways remain useful but require principled routing rather than generic averaging.\nThis ordering, 0.63 > 0.43 > 0.17, supports our central design claim: ablations that remove a\ndistinct uncertainty channel are more damaging than those that only weaken fusion over intact\npathways. Sequential variants in either direction also underperform parallel fusion (−0.30/−0.39\nPESQ), suggesting that cascading one domain through the other weakens the structural source of\npathway-specific uncertainty. Figure 3b further validates the sparse routing choice. Increasing k\nfrom 1 to 2 improves PESQ from 3.74 to 3.88 with a modest RTF of 0.28, indicating that two active\nexperts provide useful complementary inductive biases. Increasing to k=3 adds only +0.01 PESQ\nwhile increasing RTF by 25%, so we adopt top-k=2 routing as the default quality–latency trade-off.\nEfficiency and robustness. Figures 3c and 3d report efficiency and robustness. With only 8 sampling\nsteps, HybridSB-MoE achieves an RTF of 0.28 (35 ms latency), giving a 4–5× speedup over SGMSE+\nand SB-SE while surpassing their PESQ; together with ROSE-CD, our method defines the Pareto\nfrontier with a markedly higher quality ceiling. The dual-domain design is most beneficial at low\nSNR (+0.13 over ROSE-CD at 0 dB). Beyond quality, the calibration loss of Eq. (11) yields a fusionweight ECE of 0.042, an order-of-magnitude reduction from the 0.12 achieved by an uncalibrated\nsingle-pathway baseline; this is what makes uepi and uale usable as confidence signals for downstream\n8\n\n\f3.88\n\n4.0\n\nw/o uncertainty fusion 68M\n\n3.71 (-0.17)\n\n3.9\n\n3.49 (-0.39)\n\nSeq. (MoE SB) 65M\n\n3.25 (-0.63)\n\nw/o SB pathway 41M\n3.2\n\n3.4\n\n3.8\n3.7\n3.6\n\n3.45 (-0.43)\n\nw/o MoE (single expert) 23M\n\n3.0\n\nPESQ\n\n3.58 (-0.30)\n\nSeq. (SB MoE) 65M\n\n3.5\n3.6\n\nPESQ\n\n3.8\n\nPESQ\nRTF\n\n4.0\n\n3.62\n\n0.19\n\n3 exp.\nk=1\n\nROSE-CD\n\nSB-SE\n\n3.7\n3.6\n3.5\n3.4\n\nParams\n\n58M\n\n0.05\n\n63M\n\n0.1\n\n0.2\n\nSBCTM\n68M\n\n0.5\n\nPESQ\n\nPESQ\n\n3.8\n\nReal-time\nboundary\n\nOurs\n\n0.5\n\n3.89\n\n3.88\n3.74\n\n0.21\n\n0.28\n\n0.35\n\n0.4\n0.3\n0.2\n0.1\n\n5 exp.\nk=1\n\n5 exp.\nk=2\n\nExpert count and top-k\n\n5 exp.\nk=3\n\n0.0\n\n(b) Expert count, k\n\n(a) Components\n4.0 Best quality efficiency\ntrade-off\n3.9\n\nDefault\n(best trade-off)\n\nRTF\n\nFull model 68M\n\nSGMSE+\n1.0\n\nReal-Time Factor (RTF, log scale)\n\n2.0\n\n(c) Quality–RTF\n\n4.75\n4.50\n4.25\n4.00\n3.75\n3.50\n3.25\n3.00\n2.75\n\n4.48\n4.12\n3.79\n3.31\nSEMamba\nROSE-CD\nOurs\n0 dB\n\n5 dB\n\n10 dB\n\nInput SNR (dB)\n\n15 dB\n\n(d) PESQ vs. SNR\n\nFigure 3: Ablation studies and efficiency analysis. (a) Each architectural component contributes\nindependently. (b) Top-k=2 with 5 experts hits the quality–efficiency sweet spot. (c) Marker size ∝\nparameter count; dashed line is RTF=1.0. (d) PESQ across SNR levels.\ndecisions, not just as training-time auxiliaries. A scene-stratified breakdown across all 14 noise\ntypes appears in Appendix D, with PESQ standard deviation < 0.03 confirming that combinatorial\narchetype coverage generalizes across scenes without per-noise tuning.\nScope and limitations. We evaluate on VoiceBank+DEMAND, the standard SE benchmark; sceneadaptivity claims should be retested on broader corpora (DNS, WHAMR!, CHiME) where train and\ntest noise distributions diverge more substantially. Theorem 1 is a design-justifying inequality with\nworst-case constants C1 , C2 ; quantitatively fitting the predicted K −α rate against finer NFE sweeps is\na natural follow-up. The framework is single-channel; multi-channel extensions are direct (additional\ninput streams to both pathways) but beyond this paper’s scope. Finally, while the heterogeneous\narchetype set generalizes by adding archetypes per new noise family (Appendix A), automatic\narchetype discovery from data, rather than expert-designed primitives, remains open.\n\n5\n\nConclusion\n\nCombining a Schrödinger Bridge with a heterogeneous mixture-of-experts is natural; the key question\nis how to make the combination synergistic. We do so through two linked ideas. First, pathwaytyped asymmetric uncertainty fusion uses each pathway’s characteristic uncertainty signal, epistemic\ndisagreement from the spectral MoE and aleatoric variance from the waveform SB, so the mixing\nweight selects between error regimes rather than averages predictions. Second, a discretization\nbound (Theorem 1) links the small-K inference budget to two explicit training regularizers, replacing\nheuristic step-count claims with a provable objective-level guarantee. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, this\nco-design HybridSB-MoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at matched step budgets\nwhile remaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods. Three counterfactual\nablations show that no proper subset of components preserves the central design: the asymmetric\nfusion needs both a multi-expert path (for epistemic disagreement) and a stochastic bridge (for\naleatoric variance), and the small-K guarantee needs both regularizers. For SE, this suggests future\ndual-domain systems should be organized by the type of error each pathway faces, not just predictive\ncomplementarity. For generative modelling, the discretization bound shows that few-step inference\ncan be derived from training if regularizers are chosen to control the two sources of K-step error.\nFuture work will extend the framework to multi-channel input, larger benchmarks (DNS, WHAMR!,\nCHiME), and finer NFE sweeps to empirically fit the predicted K −α rate.\n\n9\n\n\fReferences\n[1] D. Wang and J. Chen, “Supervised speech separation based on deep learning: An overview,”\nIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 26, no. 10, pp.\n1702–1726, 2018.\n[2] A. Defossez, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “Real time speech enhancement in the waveform domain,”\narXiv preprint arXiv:2006.12847, 2020.\n[3] Y. Xu, J. Du, L.-R. Dai, and C.-H. Lee, “A regression approach to speech enhancement based on\ndeep neural networks,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing,\nvol. 23, no. 1, pp. 7–19, 2015.\n[4] S. Pascual, A. Bonafonte, and J. Serra, “Segan: Speech enhancement generative adversarial\nnetwork,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1703.09452, 2017.\n[5] Y. Luo and N. Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal time–frequency magnitude masking\nfor speech separation,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech and Lang. Proc., vol. 27, no. 8, p.\n1256–1266, Aug. 2019. [Online]. Available: https://doi.org/10.1109/TASLP.2019.2915167\n[6] Y. Luo, Z. Chen, and T. Yoshioka, “Dual-path rnn: efficient long sequence modeling for\ntime-domain single-channel speech separation,” in ICASSP, 2020, pp. 46–50.\n[7] J. Chen, Z. Wang, D. Tuo, Z. Wu, S. Kang, and H. Meng, “Fullsubnet+: Channel attention\nfullsubnet with complex spectrograms for speech enhancement,” in ICASSP 2022-2022 IEEE\nInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2022,\npp. 7857–7861.\n[8] Z.-Q. Wang, S. Cornell, S. Choi, Y. Lee, B.-Y. Kim, and S. Watanabe, “Tf-gridnet: Integrating\nfull- and sub-band modeling for 